WEBVTT


00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.500
Downloaded @ subs4free.club


00:01:54.582 --> 00:01:56.450
<i>As a kid in England,</i>


00:01:56.584 --> 00:01:59.787
<i>we were living in the shadows
of the Second World War.</i>


00:01:59.921 --> 00:02:01.421
<i>Your parents and your grandparents</i>


00:02:01.556 --> 00:02:03.825
<i>had been through two world wars.</i>


00:02:03.958 --> 00:02:06.426
And at the end of the Second World War,


00:02:06.561 --> 00:02:10.130
there was this great feeling
of hope towards the future.


00:02:10.263 --> 00:02:11.398
<i>In London,</i>


00:02:11.532 --> 00:02:13.801
<i>there was lots of damaged buildings</i>


00:02:13.935 --> 00:02:15.302
<i>and craters and...</i>


00:02:15.435 --> 00:02:17.905
<i>I mean, it was an apres war zone.</i>


00:02:18.039 --> 00:02:20.875
There was rationing still. I remember


00:02:21.009 --> 00:02:23.845
uh, getting eggs and
milk and butter and stuff


00:02:23.978 --> 00:02:26.614
with a little book with coupons.


00:02:26.748 --> 00:02:28.348
<i>People were recovering.</i>


00:02:28.482 --> 00:02:31.552
<i>Outside of London, in the West Midlands,</i>


00:02:31.686 --> 00:02:35.089
<i>it was a post-war,
sort of semi-suburban</i>


00:02:35.222 --> 00:02:36.323
<i>quiet life.</i>


00:02:37.625 --> 00:02:39.627
So America was my dream


00:02:39.761 --> 00:02:41.428
<i>because it was a totally different world</i>


00:02:41.562 --> 00:02:42.964
<i>to the one that we were in.</i>


00:03:25.673 --> 00:03:27.709
I was listening to this
marvelous rock and roll


00:03:27.842 --> 00:03:29.911
coming in from America.


00:03:30.044 --> 00:03:31.979
And you're hearing all this
electric guitar playing.


00:03:32.113 --> 00:03:34.549
I mean, it was almost like
it was coming from Mars,


00:03:34.682 --> 00:03:36.718
even though it was coming from Memphis.


00:03:36.851 --> 00:03:40.420
<i>But here in Britain, we were
stuck with these musicians</i>


00:03:40.555 --> 00:03:42.090
<i>who were really safe,</i>


00:03:42.222 --> 00:03:45.560
<i>until this force of nature
called Lonnie Donegan arrived.</i>


00:04:15.189 --> 00:04:17.257
<i>I was so mesmerized</i>


00:04:17.390 --> 00:04:19.861
by having seen Lonnie Donegan play.


00:04:19.994 --> 00:04:21.495
It was like a portal.


00:04:21.629 --> 00:04:23.731
It gave some access


00:04:23.865 --> 00:04:27.300
<i>to the sort of freedom that
you hadn't witnessed in England.</i>


00:04:28.503 --> 00:04:29.469
Give it up, Jimmy!


00:04:36.343 --> 00:04:40.114
So, one day I come
to school and I see...


00:04:40.248 --> 00:04:42.116
this fellow standing up in the field


00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:43.985
<i>with lots of people around him.</i>


00:04:44.118 --> 00:04:45.820
<i>His name was Rod Wyatt,</i>


00:04:45.953 --> 00:04:48.421
<i>and he's playing the
Lonnie Donegan songs</i>


00:04:48.556 --> 00:04:50.591
<i>on an acoustic guitar.</i>


00:04:50.725 --> 00:04:52.593
<i>So I went up to him and said,</i>


00:04:52.727 --> 00:04:54.629
<i>"I've got one of those at home."</i>


00:04:54.762 --> 00:04:55.797
And he said, "Bring it along


00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:57.965
"and I'll show you how to tune it up."


00:04:58.099 --> 00:05:01.202
And then he showed me
some chords as well.


00:05:39.372 --> 00:05:42.009
<i>I was absolutely
inseparable from the guitar.</i>


00:05:42.143 --> 00:05:43.678
<i>You know, I'd play it before breakfast</i>


00:05:43.811 --> 00:05:46.013
<i>and then play it on the way to school.</i>


00:05:46.147 --> 00:05:47.582
I'd go there and want to practice,


00:05:47.715 --> 00:05:49.717
but it would be confiscated


00:05:49.851 --> 00:05:52.687
and given back at the end of the day.


00:05:58.693 --> 00:06:00.460
<i>My father was like,</i>


00:06:00.595 --> 00:06:02.029
<i>"Well, I don't really understand</i>


00:06:02.163 --> 00:06:04.232
<i>all this guitar business, but...</i>


00:06:04.364 --> 00:06:05.700
<i>"I'm with you on it,</i>


00:06:05.833 --> 00:06:08.035
<i>"providing you keep
all your schoolwork up."</i>


00:06:08.169 --> 00:06:10.271
<i>But my mum was really
supportive to the idea</i>


00:06:10.403 --> 00:06:13.207
<i>of me carrying on and
starting in a group,</i>


00:06:13.341 --> 00:06:14.842
<i>and she really believed in me.</i>


00:06:14.976 --> 00:06:16.811
<i>I mean, it was really a big deal.</i>


00:06:21.849 --> 00:06:24.451
<i>My parents were in vaudeville.</i>


00:06:24.585 --> 00:06:27.221
<i>My mum was the straight
singer in the evening gown</i>


00:06:27.355 --> 00:06:28.923
<i>and my father was a comedian.</i>


00:06:29.056 --> 00:06:30.291
<i>He would play the piano.</i>


00:06:31.859 --> 00:06:33.694
<i>And so I used to travel around with them</i>


00:06:33.828 --> 00:06:34.962
<i>to all the bases,</i>


00:06:35.096 --> 00:06:36.764
<i>where they'd entertain the troops.</i>


00:06:36.898 --> 00:06:39.901
And there would be just one
international act after another.


00:06:40.034 --> 00:06:42.970
Accordion players and banjo players.


00:06:43.104 --> 00:06:44.505
<i>I took all this in.</i>


00:06:56.350 --> 00:06:59.053
<i>So I was from that musical tradition.</i>


00:06:59.186 --> 00:07:00.554
And it was all the basis


00:07:00.688 --> 00:07:02.690
for everything I ever did afterwards.


00:07:07.395 --> 00:07:09.330
<i>I grew up in the West Midlands.</i>


00:07:09.462 --> 00:07:11.866
<i>My father's bloodline is from there,</i>


00:07:11.999 --> 00:07:16.037
<i>and my mother's side of
the family are Romani Gypsy.</i>


00:07:16.170 --> 00:07:18.606
<i>My childhood was very sheltered,</i>


00:07:18.739 --> 00:07:21.676
<i>until my parents sent me to
a big school in a big town.</i>


00:07:21.809 --> 00:07:25.579
<i>Studied to be a chartered accountant.</i>


00:07:25.713 --> 00:07:28.783
<i>I mean, I'd really dug a lot
of what was going on at school,</i>


00:07:28.916 --> 00:07:30.318
<i>but when Little Richard appeared,</i>


00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:33.688
it was so provocative and all-consuming


00:07:33.821 --> 00:07:35.756
and hypnotic


00:07:35.890 --> 00:07:38.125
that it kinda knocked
everything else out of the water.


00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:56.143
I knew then that all I
wanted to do was sing.


00:08:12.492 --> 00:08:14.261
<i>I mean, that was it.</i>


00:08:14.395 --> 00:08:16.998
The syringe was in the arm. Forever.


00:08:36.550 --> 00:08:39.053
<i>Oh, well, I'm John Bonham.</i>


00:08:39.186 --> 00:08:41.622
<i>I always liked making drum
sounds when I was a kid.</i>


00:08:43.858 --> 00:08:46.293
<i>I got my first drum kit when I was ten.</i>


00:08:46.427 --> 00:08:48.729
<i>And we went to see a
film with Gene Krupa,</i>


00:08:48.863 --> 00:08:50.097
<i>and it was just amazing.</i>


00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:14.688
<i>Up here in the Midlands,</i>


00:09:14.822 --> 00:09:17.491
<i>I used to buy completely
different records, you know.</i>


00:09:17.625 --> 00:09:20.694
<i>Johnny Kidd & the Pirates
were a strong influence then.</i>


00:10:00.569 --> 00:10:02.403
<i>You heard it on all the jukeboxes.</i>


00:10:02.537 --> 00:10:05.639
Of course, they've got
those huge 15-inch speakers.


00:10:05.773 --> 00:10:08.075
That's about all you
could hear, was the bass.


00:10:10.611 --> 00:10:11.946
I heard that and thought,


00:10:12.079 --> 00:10:13.814
"That's a sound I would
really like to make."


00:10:16.884 --> 00:10:19.653
<i>When I told my father, he said,</i>


00:10:19.787 --> 00:10:22.289
"Bass guitar is a novelty instrument.


00:10:22.423 --> 00:10:25.092
"And in two years' time it
will never be heard of again.


00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:28.597
"Get yourself a saxophone,
you'll always work."


00:10:30.698 --> 00:10:32.601
So I said, "No. I want
a bass guitar, Dad."


00:10:33.602 --> 00:10:35.069
<i>He heard me practicing and went,</i>


00:10:35.202 --> 00:10:37.071
<i>"Hmm, you're coming with me.</i>


00:10:37.204 --> 00:10:39.140
"All you've got to do
is watch my left hand."


00:10:40.107 --> 00:10:42.109
On the piano. So...


00:10:42.243 --> 00:10:44.145
I learned all the standards from that.


00:10:46.647 --> 00:10:47.982
<i>I had a little band.</i>


00:10:48.115 --> 00:10:49.850
<i>We were playing at the youth club.</i>


00:10:49.984 --> 00:10:53.588
<i>And I got talking to their
very go-ahead young priest.</i>


00:10:53.721 --> 00:10:55.990
<i>He went, "We'd really like an organist."</i>


00:10:56.123 --> 00:10:59.960
I said, "Well, I could be an
organist for you, if you like."


00:11:00.094 --> 00:11:02.496
Not thinking anything of
it. He went, "Yeah. Okay."


00:11:06.635 --> 00:11:08.936
<i>In church music you have to improvise.</i>


00:11:09.069 --> 00:11:12.507
But as I wasn't very good
at playing from music anyway,


00:11:12.641 --> 00:11:14.341
I just improvised everything.


00:11:15.009 --> 00:11:16.343
I learned the hymns.


00:11:16.477 --> 00:11:18.212
I could figure out what the hymns were.


00:11:18.345 --> 00:11:21.315
I'm not particularly religious,
but it was a good gig.


00:11:21.448 --> 00:11:23.951
All right? And he was
a really cool priest.


00:11:24.084 --> 00:11:27.922
<i>And so I became organist
and choirmaster at 14.</i>


00:11:41.468 --> 00:11:44.338
Which paid me the princely
sum of £25 a year,


00:11:45.372 --> 00:11:47.041
which bought me a Fender bass.


00:11:54.381 --> 00:11:57.084
<i>I used to go and jam on Thursday night</i>


00:11:57.218 --> 00:11:58.419
<i>at the Marquee Club.</i>


00:11:58.553 --> 00:12:01.590
<i>And after a period of doing this,</i>


00:12:01.722 --> 00:12:04.191
<i>this band called Carter-Lewis
and the Southerners</i>


00:12:04.325 --> 00:12:05.459
<i>came up to me and asked me</i>


00:12:05.594 --> 00:12:07.061
<i>if I'd like to play on their record.</i>


00:12:07.194 --> 00:12:09.363
I said, "Yeah, sure.
That sounds really good."


00:12:09.496 --> 00:12:11.232
So I went along with my kit.


00:12:11.365 --> 00:12:13.602
<i>The DeArmond foot
pedal and the amplifier</i>


00:12:13.734 --> 00:12:16.270
<i>and my brand-new Gibson Les Paul.</i>


00:12:17.371 --> 00:12:18.673
<i>And the band would say,</i>


00:12:18.806 --> 00:12:21.408
<i>"Well, just make
something up for the solo."</i>


00:12:21.543 --> 00:12:24.613
By the second run-through,
I've already got a part for it.


00:12:37.758 --> 00:12:39.960
<i>And the buzz must have gone around</i>


00:12:40.094 --> 00:12:42.263
<i>about this young guitarist,</i>


00:12:42.396 --> 00:12:45.799
<i>because I started to get
calls for more studio dates.</i>


00:12:47.736 --> 00:12:49.604
<i>When I got into this session world,</i>


00:12:49.738 --> 00:12:52.674
I was, like, seven years
younger than anybody else.


00:12:52.806 --> 00:12:57.144
And they didn't have
somebody in the old guard,


00:12:57.278 --> 00:12:58.613
or the current guard,


00:12:58.747 --> 00:13:00.515
who really knew all the influences


00:13:00.649 --> 00:13:03.951
and the points of reference
that the young bands had.


00:13:04.084 --> 00:13:05.853
So, of course, I knew all of it,


00:13:05.986 --> 00:13:08.690
whether it was, like, R&B
or whether it was blues


00:13:08.822 --> 00:13:10.924
or rock, whatever it was.


00:13:11.058 --> 00:13:12.761
Everybody was getting these records


00:13:12.893 --> 00:13:14.562
that originally came over


00:13:14.696 --> 00:13:18.733
maybe with the American
merchant vessels.


00:13:18.866 --> 00:13:22.102
The R&B boom had kicked in.


00:13:34.315 --> 00:13:36.183
<i>I went to see Sonny Boy Williamson</i>


00:13:36.317 --> 00:13:38.687
<i>at the American Folk Blues Festival.</i>


00:13:38.852 --> 00:13:41.455
<i>It was around the time I was
learning to play harmonica too.</i>


00:13:41.589 --> 00:13:44.158
So it was just this amazing onslaught


00:13:44.291 --> 00:13:48.062
of music from whence we had no idea.


00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:27.702
<i>Sonny Boy was almost everything</i>


00:14:27.836 --> 00:14:32.072
that's become my whole entire
musical bloodstream really.


00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:42.216
But in my own hometown,
I had my own hero,


00:14:42.983 --> 00:14:44.985
and it was Perry Foster.


00:14:45.119 --> 00:14:49.223
<i>And he had this kind of setup
called the Delta Blues Band.</i>


00:14:49.356 --> 00:14:52.594
<i>And, um, somehow or another,
I managed to blag my way</i>


00:14:52.727 --> 00:14:54.161
into this band.


00:14:55.963 --> 00:14:57.931
<i>I was prepared to be a mod or a rocker</i>


00:14:58.065 --> 00:15:00.568
<i>or a beatnik or anything
so long as I could sing</i>


00:15:00.702 --> 00:15:03.270
<i>and gather these looks around me,</i>


00:15:03.404 --> 00:15:05.774
just trying to make
it stick on the wall.


00:15:08.242 --> 00:15:10.712
<i>I used to play and I used
to have a job as well.</i>


00:15:10.845 --> 00:15:13.782
<i>I used to be working with my
father in the building trade.</i>


00:15:13.914 --> 00:15:16.950
<i>Used to be good builders then.</i>


00:15:17.084 --> 00:15:18.620
<i>But, you know, the
job gradually gets less</i>


00:15:18.753 --> 00:15:21.488
<i>and the music more, you know.
That's what seems to happen.</i>


00:15:21.623 --> 00:15:24.124
<i>You know, we left school
and we formed small groups,</i>


00:15:24.258 --> 00:15:25.527
<i>and things like that,</i>


00:15:25.660 --> 00:15:28.295
<i>and played locally and everything.</i>


00:15:28.429 --> 00:15:31.198
<i>You know, for me, soul music
was a strong influence then.</i>


00:15:56.558 --> 00:15:58.425
<i>James Brown was a big favorite</i>


00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:01.328
<i>because, you know, the
drum sound was just amazing.</i>


00:16:01.462 --> 00:16:03.263
<i>I thought, "I'm gonna get that sound."</i>


00:16:12.372 --> 00:16:13.608
<i>When I was 16,</i>


00:16:13.741 --> 00:16:16.644
<i>I went to see a group
with Robert singing.</i>


00:16:16.778 --> 00:16:20.682
<i>The gig went incredibly well
because he was bloody great.</i>


00:16:20.815 --> 00:16:22.382
<i>In the crowd watching this</i>


00:16:22.517 --> 00:16:27.054
<i>was some larger-than-life guy
with his beautiful girlfriend.</i>


00:16:27.187 --> 00:16:29.356
<i>He got a hold of me
afterwards and he said,</i>


00:16:29.490 --> 00:16:31.058
<i>"Well, you're okay,</i>


00:16:31.191 --> 00:16:34.361
"but you'd be a lot better with
a proper drummer behind you."


00:16:34.495 --> 00:16:37.364
And I thought, "Jesus
Christ. Here we go."


00:16:39.333 --> 00:16:41.168
<i>The first time we played together</i>


00:16:41.301 --> 00:16:42.336
<i>we were about 16.</i>


00:16:42.469 --> 00:16:43.638
<i>And it was really good.</i>


00:16:51.178 --> 00:16:52.881
<i>John was amazing.</i>


00:16:53.013 --> 00:16:54.883
<i>He was a really powerful drummer.</i>


00:16:56.116 --> 00:16:58.786
I watched him with absolute amazement.


00:16:58.920 --> 00:17:02.590
He was just fantastic.


00:17:02.724 --> 00:17:05.459
<i>But it's so competitive
playing locally anyway.</i>


00:17:05.593 --> 00:17:08.028
<i>You'd, like, have a
time going on the road</i>


00:17:08.161 --> 00:17:09.597
<i>and everything would be roses,</i>


00:17:09.731 --> 00:17:11.900
<i>and then all of a sudden,
no more gigs, no more money.</i>


00:17:12.032 --> 00:17:13.902
<i>You're back to where
you started, you know.</i>


00:17:14.034 --> 00:17:17.070
One time, we were running out of fuel


00:17:17.204 --> 00:17:19.874
and I had to siphon
gasoline from another car


00:17:20.008 --> 00:17:22.544
and I was caught by the police
in the middle of the night,


00:17:22.677 --> 00:17:25.112
sucking a piece of rubber into a can.


00:17:25.245 --> 00:17:28.282
And him sitting in the back shivering,


00:17:28.415 --> 00:17:30.417
thinking, "What the hell will Pat say?"


00:17:32.219 --> 00:17:35.155
<i>Pat became his wife,
and she used to say,</i>


00:17:35.289 --> 00:17:38.492
"Don't you dare play with that Planty.


00:17:38.626 --> 00:17:40.093
"He's a complete disaster."


00:17:49.336 --> 00:17:52.339
<i>I left school at 16.</i>


00:17:52.472 --> 00:17:54.374
I failed most of my exams


00:17:54.509 --> 00:17:56.310
<i>because I'd been playing
all night in some club</i>


00:17:56.443 --> 00:17:58.546
<i>the night before my O-Levels.</i>


00:17:58.680 --> 00:18:00.048
<i>And my dad was saying,</i>


00:18:00.180 --> 00:18:03.051
"Well, I can get you a
job as a clerk somewhere."


00:18:04.384 --> 00:18:06.721
"No, no. I'll find a band,
Dad. I'll find a band."


00:18:06.854 --> 00:18:08.388
So there used to be a place in London


00:18:08.523 --> 00:18:09.791
called Archer Street.


00:18:09.924 --> 00:18:11.826
<i>And every Monday morning,</i>


00:18:11.960 --> 00:18:15.429
<i>all the local musicians
would congregate for work.</i>


00:18:16.330 --> 00:18:18.733
And so I went there as a kid.


00:18:18.866 --> 00:18:20.467
<i>Every Monday morning, I was there.</i>


00:18:35.215 --> 00:18:37.619
<i>And I started getting booked with bands</i>


00:18:37.752 --> 00:18:39.453
<i>and in the session scene.</i>


00:18:43.091 --> 00:18:46.861
<i>Here comes Jimmy Page
with his electric guitar.</i>


00:18:46.995 --> 00:18:49.129
<i>That's my Les Paul, my Black Beauty.</i>


00:18:49.262 --> 00:18:51.799
<i>And this guitar was the guitar</i>


00:18:51.933 --> 00:18:53.768
<i>that I played most of my sessions on.</i>


00:18:55.903 --> 00:18:57.805
<i>There we are. Going in there to see</i>


00:18:57.939 --> 00:19:00.942
<i>what I'm gonna be faced
with on that music stand.</i>


00:19:13.621 --> 00:19:16.724
Sessions were a lot of fun
in those days, actually.


00:19:16.858 --> 00:19:19.027
<i>When I started, I ended up
playing a lot of sessions</i>


00:19:19.159 --> 00:19:20.394
together with Jimmy.


00:19:22.964 --> 00:19:25.700
<i>The Goldfinger session
was at Abbey Road.</i>


00:19:25.833 --> 00:19:28.102
<i>Jimmy and I were in the rhythm section.</i>


00:19:28.235 --> 00:19:30.004
<i>There was a full orchestra</i>


00:19:30.138 --> 00:19:31.338
and Shirley Bassey.


00:19:35.543 --> 00:19:38.211
<i>The whole energy...
Just to be in there...</i>


00:19:38.345 --> 00:19:41.149
I mean, you know, I'm
still a kid, really.


00:19:41.281 --> 00:19:43.785
And it's, "Wow. It's Shirley Bassey."


00:19:43.918 --> 00:19:46.087
<i>While you were playing,
you could see her.</i>


00:19:46.253 --> 00:19:48.488
And she did the whole thing.


00:19:49.657 --> 00:19:51.592
Like that. It was wonderful to watch.


00:20:12.046 --> 00:20:16.117
<i>These studios would work
with ruthless efficiency.</i>


00:20:16.249 --> 00:20:18.218
<i>So you couldn't be the one to mess up</i>


00:20:18.351 --> 00:20:19.954
<i>so they went into overtime.</i>


00:20:20.088 --> 00:20:21.522
Of course, if you messed up,


00:20:21.656 --> 00:20:23.191
you wouldn't have been seen again.


00:20:35.937 --> 00:20:37.872
There'd be, like, a
pocket diary, like this,


00:20:38.005 --> 00:20:40.608
that I'd be carrying with me.


00:20:40.742 --> 00:20:43.878
I played on sessions
with the Rolling Stones,


00:20:44.011 --> 00:20:45.947
David Bowie, Petula Clark...


00:20:46.080 --> 00:20:48.116
I was on the Kinks' records.


00:20:48.248 --> 00:20:50.151
The first Who record.


00:20:50.283 --> 00:20:52.954
<i>I mean, it goes right across the board.</i>


00:20:53.087 --> 00:20:55.056
But I didn't do anything
with the Beatles.


00:21:22.717 --> 00:21:24.919
<i>I did quite a lot of work with Donovan</i>


00:21:25.052 --> 00:21:28.222
<i>and I really enjoyed working with him.</i>


00:21:28.355 --> 00:21:30.958
<i>And Mickie Most was his producer.</i>


00:21:33.561 --> 00:21:36.197
<i>These sessions were
great 'cause I could watch</i>


00:21:36.329 --> 00:21:38.933
and I could ask questions about things,


00:21:39.066 --> 00:21:40.268
and I certainly did.


00:21:40.400 --> 00:21:43.070
I made it my business to ask engineers


00:21:43.204 --> 00:21:46.641
<i>how certain effects were done,
like tape echo and reverb.</i>


00:21:46.774 --> 00:21:49.442
<i>And you could see the
way that they were miking</i>


00:21:49.577 --> 00:21:51.712
<i>various instruments.</i>


00:21:51.846 --> 00:21:53.648
<i>And, basically, that's what I could do.</i>


00:21:53.781 --> 00:21:55.550
I was allowed to sort of do that.


00:21:55.683 --> 00:21:58.085
Not only that, I got a
chance to have a solo as well.


00:21:58.219 --> 00:21:59.554
So that was good.


00:22:09.831 --> 00:22:10.965
<i>I went up to Mickie Most</i>


00:22:11.098 --> 00:22:12.432
<i>and said, "Do you need an arranger?"</i>


00:22:12.567 --> 00:22:14.936
"You do..." "Yeah, I do arrangements.


00:22:15.069 --> 00:22:17.638
"Yeah. All the time." And...


00:22:17.772 --> 00:22:19.941
I hadn't really done that before.


00:22:20.074 --> 00:22:24.411
<i>But my dad had always taught
me never turn down work, right?</i>


00:22:24.545 --> 00:22:27.982
<i>And had a great working
relationship with him.</i>


00:22:29.617 --> 00:22:32.320
<i>He used to drive by in
his yellow Rolls-Royce</i>


00:22:32.452 --> 00:22:35.223
<i>and drop an acetate through my letterbox</i>


00:22:35.355 --> 00:22:39.861
with "You know what we want.
It's Friday." And that was it.


00:22:39.994 --> 00:22:42.697
<i>I'd be playing bass in sessions
all day, seven days a week.</i>


00:22:42.830 --> 00:22:44.665
<i>So I had to write arrangements at night.</i>


00:22:44.799 --> 00:22:46.466
<i>And then nine o'clock in the morning</i>


00:22:46.601 --> 00:22:49.036
I'd go to the session and do the songs.


00:22:49.170 --> 00:22:52.173
<i>I did all the arrangements
for Herman's Hermits</i>


00:22:52.306 --> 00:22:54.175
<i>and Donovan,</i>


00:22:54.308 --> 00:22:56.544
<i>and had quite a lot to do with Lulu.</i>


00:22:56.677 --> 00:22:57.912
<i>'Cause I did all her arrangements</i>


00:22:58.045 --> 00:22:59.013
<i>with Mickie Most as well.</i>


00:23:14.962 --> 00:23:16.664
<i>I even did film work with her,</i>


00:23:16.797 --> 00:23:20.902
<i>like To Sir, With Love, which
was a big hit in America.</i>


00:23:21.035 --> 00:23:23.604
<i>This was considered the
pinnacle of the music business.</i>


00:23:24.772 --> 00:23:27.108
<i>My dad was incredibly proud</i>


00:23:27.241 --> 00:23:29.143
when people he knew and respected


00:23:29.277 --> 00:23:31.779
would come... "I saw your
John on so-and-so session


00:23:31.913 --> 00:23:34.447
"the other day. He's
okay. He's good, you know."


00:23:34.582 --> 00:23:36.117
And...


00:23:36.250 --> 00:23:37.417
Puffed him up a lot.


00:23:41.322 --> 00:23:43.124
<i>I was in a lot of different bands</i>


00:23:43.257 --> 00:23:44.792
<i>around that time.</i>


00:23:44.926 --> 00:23:47.762
<i>And I did a couple adventures
in recording studios.</i>


00:23:47.895 --> 00:23:52.266
But my parents, they
weren't that keen at all.


00:23:52.400 --> 00:23:57.071
In fact, when I cut my record,
the first single on CBS,


00:23:57.204 --> 00:24:01.609
they didn't know anything
about it, 'cause I, um...


00:24:01.742 --> 00:24:04.946
I was given the alternative
of staying in the world of


00:24:05.079 --> 00:24:06.380
academia and...


00:24:07.548 --> 00:24:10.017
my article... Training for my articles


00:24:11.719 --> 00:24:13.821
as a chartered accountant or...


00:24:15.323 --> 00:24:17.625
you just go out into the
world and do what you gotta do.


00:24:17.758 --> 00:24:19.492
So I did.


00:24:19.627 --> 00:24:21.729
So I waved everybody goodbye.


00:24:29.603 --> 00:24:30.871
<i>I knew there were singers</i>


00:24:31.005 --> 00:24:32.306
<i>who had more chops than me,</i>


00:24:32.440 --> 00:24:37.178
but I was just out there,
throwing it up in the air.


00:24:37.311 --> 00:24:42.383
I had caftans, painted faces,
bells ringing, incense blazing,


00:24:42.516 --> 00:24:44.819
...and was just...


00:24:44.952 --> 00:24:47.388
<i>Robert used to get into
more trouble and mischief</i>


00:24:47.521 --> 00:24:49.590
<i>than any other friends
I have up here, you know.</i>


00:24:49.724 --> 00:24:51.959
<i>It's ridiculous, you know.</i>


00:24:52.093 --> 00:24:56.797
John came up to me and said,
"Well, what's all that about?"


00:24:56.931 --> 00:25:00.034
<i>So I said, "Yeah, you're
right. What is that all about?"</i>


00:25:00.167 --> 00:25:01.736
<i>It was the end of '67.</i>


00:25:01.869 --> 00:25:03.537
<i>We got back together</i>


00:25:03.671 --> 00:25:05.873
<i>and the playing went incredibly well.</i>


00:25:21.489 --> 00:25:23.724
<i>So we formed the Band of Joy.</i>


00:25:23.858 --> 00:25:26.460
We wanted to mix the
blues with psychedelia.


00:25:26.594 --> 00:25:29.330
We wanted to be a part
of that huge movement.


00:25:29.463 --> 00:25:30.998
We played at Frank Freeman's.


00:25:31.132 --> 00:25:34.101
<i>We played Middle Earth in London.</i>


00:25:34.235 --> 00:25:37.638
<i>We went down to Tin Pan Alley
and we recorded Memory Lane,</i>


00:25:37.772 --> 00:25:40.641
<i>which was my first
adventure in songwriting.</i>


00:25:41.842 --> 00:25:44.178
<i>Sadly, we never got it released.</i>


00:25:44.311 --> 00:25:45.913
And not many people liked us.


00:25:46.515 --> 00:25:48.582
But we liked us.


00:26:16.577 --> 00:26:18.446
<i>Pat was screaming at John, saying,</i>


00:26:18.579 --> 00:26:20.815
"I told you to stay away from him.


00:26:20.948 --> 00:26:22.183
"He'll get you nowhere."


00:26:22.316 --> 00:26:24.785
And so he went off with Tim Rose


00:26:24.919 --> 00:26:27.655
<i>and played I Got a
Loneliness and Morning Dew.</i>


00:26:27.788 --> 00:26:29.924
<i>That's how it used to go, really.</i>


00:26:30.057 --> 00:26:31.158
<i>I mean, we had a kid then.</i>


00:26:31.292 --> 00:26:32.693
<i>So you'd end up having to get a job</i>


00:26:32.827 --> 00:26:34.428
<i>in a professional group to
get a little bit of money</i>


00:26:34.563 --> 00:26:37.164
<i>to sort of live with, really.</i>


00:26:37.298 --> 00:26:40.935
I just fell from favor everywhere.


00:26:42.336 --> 00:26:44.472
Things were pretty tough for me then.


00:26:44.605 --> 00:26:48.476
I had a brown suitcase
and some penicillin,


00:26:48.609 --> 00:26:50.277
and I was homeless.
I had nowhere to live.


00:26:50.411 --> 00:26:51.412
So I...


00:26:52.913 --> 00:26:55.416
I got a gig with this
band called Obs-Tweedle.


00:27:06.393 --> 00:27:08.496
I was totally accepted


00:27:08.629 --> 00:27:11.432
<i>in the world of being a studio musician.</i>


00:27:11.566 --> 00:27:14.635
<i>I had been caught up doing the things</i>


00:27:14.768 --> 00:27:18.005
<i>that all of the real old-timers
when I joined in were doing.</i>


00:27:18.139 --> 00:27:19.373
Like Muzak.


00:27:19.508 --> 00:27:22.476
Muzak is what they would
call, like, lift music.


00:27:22.611 --> 00:27:24.311
You know? Um...


00:27:24.445 --> 00:27:28.115
Very, very difficult to do


00:27:28.249 --> 00:27:29.416
a Muzak session,


00:27:29.551 --> 00:27:31.285
'cause you'd have a whole ream of music


00:27:31.418 --> 00:27:33.522
and you'd have to keep
turning it and playing it,


00:27:33.687 --> 00:27:34.989
and turning it and playing it.


00:27:35.122 --> 00:27:37.526
To do that, to actually
get halfway through


00:27:37.658 --> 00:27:40.494
without being physically
sick was quite a miracle.


00:27:40.629 --> 00:27:43.797
So I knew


00:27:43.931 --> 00:27:46.367
that it was time to sort
of come out of there.


00:27:46.500 --> 00:27:49.970
All my friends, like Eric
and Jeff, were all in bands


00:27:50.104 --> 00:27:51.739
and they were having a great time.


00:27:51.872 --> 00:27:53.874
And I thought, "I've got
a lot that I really want


00:27:54.008 --> 00:27:55.610
"to be able to offer."


00:27:56.443 --> 00:27:57.945
<i>And Jeff Beck said,</i>


00:27:58.078 --> 00:28:00.714
<i>"It'd be really good if you
came in with the Yardbirds."</i>


00:28:00.848 --> 00:28:02.950
So I thought, "How would
you really like to play


00:28:03.083 --> 00:28:04.553
"if you had the chance?"


00:28:04.685 --> 00:28:07.556
"Right. You do it. Do it now.
This is your opportunity."


00:28:33.847 --> 00:28:35.950
Going into the Yardbirds with Jeff,


00:28:36.083 --> 00:28:37.184
it was touring with a friend


00:28:37.318 --> 00:28:39.621
and having, you know,
a really good time.


00:28:39.753 --> 00:28:42.089
<i>But in the middle of an American tour,</i>


00:28:42.223 --> 00:28:45.826
<i>he left the band, and
I was quite shocked.</i>


00:28:45.960 --> 00:28:50.599
<i>But it gave me the opportunity
to try a totally new direction.</i>


00:28:50.731 --> 00:28:53.367
<i>So I took over on lead guitar.</i>


00:29:00.908 --> 00:29:03.210
<i>Nobody was interested in the Yardbirds</i>


00:29:03.344 --> 00:29:05.079
<i>over here in England anymore.</i>


00:29:05.212 --> 00:29:08.550
<i>So we started to really grow
our underground following</i>


00:29:08.683 --> 00:29:10.484
<i>in America.</i>


00:29:10.619 --> 00:29:14.488
And it was just a thrill,
an absolute thrill,


00:29:14.623 --> 00:29:17.958
<i>just really getting a feel
for the West Coast music scene.</i>


00:29:25.299 --> 00:29:27.167
<i>But we were up against Mickie Most,</i>


00:29:27.301 --> 00:29:29.136
<i>who was our producer,</i>


00:29:29.270 --> 00:29:31.640
<i>so we were recording
all these awful singles</i>


00:29:31.772 --> 00:29:35.075
for the AM radio,
which was the pop radio,


00:29:35.209 --> 00:29:38.345
<i>and I didn't want to be
making singles anymore.</i>


00:29:39.780 --> 00:29:41.148
<i>Being in America</i>


00:29:41.282 --> 00:29:43.652
<i>and seeing the advent of
the FM underground radio,</i>


00:29:43.784 --> 00:29:46.420
<i>which was playing
whole sides of albums...</i>


00:29:46.554 --> 00:29:50.592
I knew that this was gonna be
the way to go in the future.


00:29:50.725 --> 00:29:52.359
<i>So that was my plan.</i>


00:29:52.493 --> 00:29:54.663
To make albums that would be played,


00:29:54.795 --> 00:29:57.498
a whole side, on the FM stations.


00:30:04.773 --> 00:30:06.741
<i>I had this new sound for the Yardbirds</i>


00:30:06.874 --> 00:30:08.543
<i>already worked out in my head.</i>


00:30:08.677 --> 00:30:12.112
I already knew exactly what I
wanted to be doing, guitar-wise,


00:30:12.246 --> 00:30:14.048
<i>on the new stuff that I had in mind,</i>


00:30:14.181 --> 00:30:15.449
<i>like Dazed and Confused,</i>


00:30:15.583 --> 00:30:18.452
<i>which was inspired by Jake Holmes.</i>


00:30:43.110 --> 00:30:47.214
<i>That guitar was given
to me by Jeff Beck.</i>


00:30:47.348 --> 00:30:50.217
<i>I painted it. I consecrated the guitar.</i>


00:30:51.586 --> 00:30:55.724
This is the guitar that
takes the full journey,


00:30:55.856 --> 00:30:59.893
like Excalibur, the mythical sword.


00:31:14.843 --> 00:31:17.911
<i>I went to a palm reading in LA.</i>


00:31:18.847 --> 00:31:20.981
<i>And the palmist said,</i>


00:31:21.115 --> 00:31:24.552
"You're gonna be making
a decision very soon


00:31:24.686 --> 00:31:26.920
"which is gonna change your life."


00:31:27.054 --> 00:31:31.091
A meeting happened, like, two
or three days after the palmist,


00:31:31.225 --> 00:31:32.359
where the rest of the group said,


00:31:32.493 --> 00:31:33.695
"That's it. We wanna fold."


00:31:35.530 --> 00:31:36.731
<i>I mean, it was a shock.</i>


00:31:39.967 --> 00:31:43.772
<i>I immediately went, "Well,
now I know what I'm gonna do.</i>


00:31:43.904 --> 00:31:45.305
"I'm gonna form my own group,


00:31:45.439 --> 00:31:48.242
"and I know exactly what it
is that I wanna do with it,"


00:31:48.375 --> 00:31:51.713
<i>and I just needed to
find a powerful vocalist.</i>


00:31:51.846 --> 00:31:53.947
<i>And so there was a
singer called Terry Reid.</i>


00:31:54.081 --> 00:31:55.517
<i>He registered with me</i>


00:31:55.650 --> 00:31:58.485
<i>and I thought, "I'm gonna aim for him."</i>


00:31:58.620 --> 00:32:02.590
<i>I asked Peter Grant if he
could track down Terry Reid.</i>


00:32:02.724 --> 00:32:04.958
<i>Peter Grant was the
manager of the Yardbirds,</i>


00:32:05.092 --> 00:32:07.060
and, bless him, he
really believed in me.


00:32:07.194 --> 00:32:09.229
This is the thing, that
once the Yardbirds folded,


00:32:09.363 --> 00:32:10.899
and I said, "Well, I
wanna start a group,"


00:32:11.031 --> 00:32:13.668
he was there. You know?
He said, "Great," you know?


00:32:13.802 --> 00:32:15.804
And "Whatever help I can give."


00:32:15.936 --> 00:32:19.774
Peter Grant and Mickie Most
were in the same office,


00:32:19.908 --> 00:32:21.576
and they had two desks,


00:32:21.709 --> 00:32:24.746
and they more or less faced
each other across the room.


00:32:26.079 --> 00:32:28.015
<i>The next thing that I hear is,</i>


00:32:28.148 --> 00:32:31.151
<i>"Unfortunately Terry
Reid's signed a solo deal</i>


00:32:31.285 --> 00:32:32.286
<i>"with Mickie Most."</i>


00:32:32.419 --> 00:32:34.221
And I... "Oh, yeah?"


00:32:34.354 --> 00:32:35.757
- Off the press?
- Right. I know that one.


00:32:35.890 --> 00:32:37.090
- For you.
- Right.


00:32:37.224 --> 00:32:38.626
I thought, "Wow. This
is super ruthless."


00:32:38.760 --> 00:32:41.061
But nevertheless, Terry Reid suggested


00:32:41.195 --> 00:32:43.598
<i>a singer from the Midlands
in a band called Obs-Tweedle.</i>


00:32:43.731 --> 00:32:45.032
<i>And I thought,</i>


00:32:45.165 --> 00:32:47.301
<i>"This is an unusual
name. This is a bit odd."</i>


00:32:47.434 --> 00:32:50.971
He and Peter Grant came
up to have a look at me.


00:32:51.104 --> 00:32:54.141
He asked me if I knew where
he could find Robert Plant.


00:32:55.375 --> 00:32:58.111
And I said, "Yeah. Right here."


00:32:58.245 --> 00:33:01.982
He was doing some
wonderful improvised vocals


00:33:02.115 --> 00:33:03.551
<i>and I thought they were marvelous.</i>


00:33:03.685 --> 00:33:06.353
<i>So I invited him round my house.</i>


00:33:06.487 --> 00:33:09.056
I still had nowhere to live, sadly.


00:33:09.189 --> 00:33:12.493
<i>So I took my suitcase and
got a train to Pangbourne</i>


00:33:12.627 --> 00:33:14.294
<i>and knocked on another door.</i>


00:33:15.763 --> 00:33:17.699
<i>Yeah. We had a really good connection.</i>


00:33:17.832 --> 00:33:20.902
And, uh, I think he was sort
of looking through my records...


00:33:21.034 --> 00:33:22.369
"I've got that one." You know?


00:33:22.504 --> 00:33:24.939
<i>And I played him a Joan Baez version</i>


00:33:25.072 --> 00:33:26.541
<i>of Babe I'm Gonna Leave You</i>


00:33:26.674 --> 00:33:30.143
<i>and said, "Well, if you can
sing that top line there,</i>


00:33:30.277 --> 00:33:32.145
<i>"I've got an arrangement
that goes with it."</i>


00:33:32.279 --> 00:33:35.249
And it just really,
really dovetailed together.


00:33:35.382 --> 00:33:36.416
It was beautiful.


00:33:36.551 --> 00:33:39.319
He just sang beautifully.


00:33:51.566 --> 00:33:53.835
We knew something was in the air.


00:33:55.035 --> 00:33:58.405
I could feel this stuff around me.


00:33:58.540 --> 00:34:02.442
<i>Jimmy's guitar playing was
just tremendous. Amazing.</i>


00:34:05.713 --> 00:34:06.848
<i>Jimmy then said to me,</i>


00:34:06.981 --> 00:34:09.082
<i>"Well, we've got this drummer lined up."</i>


00:34:09.216 --> 00:34:11.686
And before he went any
further, I said, "Wait a minute.


00:34:11.819 --> 00:34:13.655
"There is no drummer on the planet


00:34:13.788 --> 00:34:16.558
"to compare with John Bonham.


00:34:16.691 --> 00:34:18.358
<i>"He's magnificent."</i>


00:34:18.492 --> 00:34:20.562
<i>He was playing with Tim Rose.</i>


00:34:20.695 --> 00:34:22.195
<i>Jimmy said, "Well, if that's the case,</i>


00:34:22.329 --> 00:34:23.765
<i>"let's go and see him."</i>


00:34:23.898 --> 00:34:27.301
And I said, "John, you've
got to listen to Jimmy play.


00:34:27.434 --> 00:34:30.905
"He's amazing." John
said, "Not a chance."


00:34:31.039 --> 00:34:32.874
I said, "You gotta be crazy.


00:34:33.007 --> 00:34:37.077
"You don't wanna do this
stuff, playing electric folk."


00:34:37.210 --> 00:34:39.747
"Come with me." He said,
"Pat would kill me."


00:34:39.881 --> 00:34:41.248
"Don't say that."


00:34:41.381 --> 00:34:43.450
I said, "I'll talk
to Pat, and your mom."


00:34:46.320 --> 00:34:50.490
<i>My wife Mo read that
"Jimmy's forming a band.</i>


00:34:50.625 --> 00:34:52.426
<i>"You should give him a call.</i>


00:34:52.560 --> 00:34:53.895
"See if he wants a bass player."


00:34:54.028 --> 00:34:55.128
Like, "No. I can't...


00:34:55.262 --> 00:34:57.065
"I've got this to do,
I've got that to do.


00:34:57.197 --> 00:34:58.800
"I got these... and albums..."


00:34:58.933 --> 00:35:00.768
"No," she said. She just insisted.


00:35:00.902 --> 00:35:04.237
"Give him a call, please." So I did.


00:35:04.371 --> 00:35:07.207
And I said, "Hey, Jim.
Heard you're forming a band."


00:35:07.341 --> 00:35:08.576
He went, "Yeah, yeah."


00:35:08.710 --> 00:35:10.712
I said, "Do you want a bass player?"


00:35:10.845 --> 00:35:12.614
He went, "Well, actually, yes.


00:35:12.747 --> 00:35:14.782
"But there's a problem."
I said, "What's that?"


00:35:14.916 --> 00:35:17.451
He said, "Well, the drummer
is playing with Tim Rose


00:35:17.585 --> 00:35:20.521
"and he's getting 40 quid
a week. Can we best it?"


00:35:23.123 --> 00:35:25.192
So we decided, yeah, we probably could.


00:35:27.061 --> 00:35:30.497
<i>Peter Grant managed to get
a two-hour rehearsal space</i>


00:35:30.632 --> 00:35:32.499
<i>in Gerrard Street.</i>


00:35:32.634 --> 00:35:36.504
<i>So we all get there and
I've got my equipment set up.</i>


00:35:36.638 --> 00:35:39.406
John Paul Jones is there
with his session equipment,


00:35:39.540 --> 00:35:41.509
uh, and John's got his drums.


00:35:41.643 --> 00:35:44.277
<i>It literally was just
wall to wall amplifiers</i>


00:35:44.411 --> 00:35:46.413
<i>and we just picked one up...</i>


00:35:46.547 --> 00:35:48.816
"Hello. How are you?" "Right." You know?


00:35:48.950 --> 00:35:51.351
"I'm so-and-so.
He's so-and-so else."


00:35:51.485 --> 00:35:52.820
<i>It was quite strange, really,</i>


00:35:52.954 --> 00:35:54.221
<i>meeting John Paul and Jimmy.</i>


00:35:54.354 --> 00:35:56.256
<i>You know, coming from where
I'd come from, you know,</i>


00:35:56.390 --> 00:35:58.126
<i>I'd sort of thought, you
know, it was a bit of a joke,</i>


00:35:58.258 --> 00:36:00.227
<i>getting telegrams and things, you know.</i>


00:36:00.360 --> 00:36:02.630
<i>There's a chance of becoming
one of the Yardbirds.</i>


00:36:02.764 --> 00:36:05.033
<i>It's like a gift from heaven, wasn't it?</i>


00:36:05.165 --> 00:36:07.969
I said the first
number that I want to do


00:36:08.102 --> 00:36:09.369
<i>is Train Kept A-Rollin'.</i>


00:36:09.504 --> 00:36:11.438
<i>Train Kept A-Rollin'. "Do you know it?"</i>


00:36:11.572 --> 00:36:14.509
I went, "Nope." He said,
"Well, it's a 12-bar."


00:36:15.877 --> 00:36:17.879
"It's got this riff."
"Okay. Count it in."


00:36:18.012 --> 00:36:19.814
And that was it.


00:36:19.947 --> 00:36:21.649
And the room just exploded.


00:36:21.783 --> 00:36:24.217
We just kept playing it
and doing, like, solo breaks


00:36:24.351 --> 00:36:27.088
and all the rest of it,
and Robert's improvising.


00:36:27.220 --> 00:36:28.956
I'd never heard anything like it.


00:36:29.090 --> 00:36:30.725
I was expecting some cool soul singer


00:36:30.858 --> 00:36:34.261
and there's this screaming maniac


00:36:34.394 --> 00:36:36.931
with this fantastic voice
and a fantastic range.


00:36:37.065 --> 00:36:38.866
You know, I was like, "What
are you doing up there?


00:36:39.000 --> 00:36:40.668
"You'll hurt yourself, man."


00:36:40.802 --> 00:36:43.905
It was devastating
because it seemed like


00:36:45.039 --> 00:36:46.974
that had been what I'd been waiting for.


00:36:47.108 --> 00:36:48.576
<i>I was pretty shy.</i>


00:36:48.710 --> 00:36:51.211
<i>The best thing to do when
you're in a situation like that</i>


00:36:51.344 --> 00:36:53.548
<i>is not to say much
and just soldier along</i>


00:36:53.681 --> 00:36:55.348
<i>and suss it all out, sort of thing.</i>


00:36:55.482 --> 00:36:56.951
Well, being a bass player,


00:36:57.085 --> 00:36:59.020
and he was probably
thinking the same thing,


00:36:59.153 --> 00:37:00.621
you know, because rhythm sections


00:37:00.755 --> 00:37:03.423
are very sensitive creatures really...


00:37:04.324 --> 00:37:06.027
Um...


00:37:06.160 --> 00:37:09.731
I just needed to know that
we could work together.


00:37:09.864 --> 00:37:11.999
<i>The first time we played
together, you know,</i>


00:37:12.133 --> 00:37:14.001
<i>you could tell it was
gonna be a good group.</i>


00:37:14.135 --> 00:37:15.703
<i>You know, not being
sort of flashy about it.</i>


00:37:17.705 --> 00:37:19.239
<i>But I am.</i>


00:37:21.008 --> 00:37:22.342
Marvelous.


00:37:22.476 --> 00:37:26.246
When it finally came to a
halt, I'm absolutely convinced


00:37:26.379 --> 00:37:29.917
that everyone knew that that
was a life-changing experience.


00:37:30.051 --> 00:37:31.986
And I think there was
so much kinetic energy,


00:37:32.120 --> 00:37:35.089
so much energy that needed
to come out for everybody.


00:37:35.223 --> 00:37:36.456
"Let's take it to the next stage


00:37:36.591 --> 00:37:38.059
"and let's take it to Pangbourne."


00:37:49.570 --> 00:37:51.906
<i>Pangbourne is on the River Thames.</i>


00:37:52.039 --> 00:37:55.076
<i>I remember when I first
came across this boat house,</i>


00:37:55.209 --> 00:37:57.779
<i>I just thought, "It's absolute magic."</i>


00:37:57.912 --> 00:37:59.479
<i>It had a good energy to it.</i>


00:37:59.614 --> 00:38:03.251
<i>And it was just a
cauldron of creativity.</i>


00:38:03.383 --> 00:38:04.652
<i>And this is the house that I lived in</i>


00:38:04.786 --> 00:38:07.922
<i>in 1967 through to 1970.</i>


00:38:08.055 --> 00:38:11.324
We set up initially in
the upstairs of the house.


00:38:11.458 --> 00:38:13.227
Where those windows are at the top,


00:38:13.360 --> 00:38:14.461
there's a largish room


00:38:14.595 --> 00:38:17.464
and that was where
we did the rehearsals.


00:38:23.403 --> 00:38:26.607
We were overlooking the
river. It was beautiful.


00:38:26.741 --> 00:38:28.943
<i>And we just got to know each other</i>


00:38:29.076 --> 00:38:30.278
<i>and worked pretty hard.</i>


00:38:30.410 --> 00:38:31.478
<i>We weren't slackers.</i>


00:38:31.612 --> 00:38:33.346
Our aim wasn't to be seen on the telly.


00:38:33.480 --> 00:38:36.284
We all wanted to play music very badly.


00:38:36.416 --> 00:38:39.554
We were working on the
material for the album,


00:38:39.687 --> 00:38:42.690
and then we were putting
in other material as well


00:38:42.824 --> 00:38:44.592
that was gonna fill out the set.


00:38:45.626 --> 00:38:48.162
<i>We were rehearsing for days,</i>


00:38:48.296 --> 00:38:50.665
<i>and it would have been pretty loud.</i>


00:38:50.798 --> 00:38:52.767
<i>You'd hear it. You'd hear it over here.</i>


00:38:52.900 --> 00:38:55.636
And I did expect somebody
to be knocking on the door


00:38:55.770 --> 00:38:57.370
<i>to complain, but nobody did.</i>


00:39:05.513 --> 00:39:07.248
<i>We got together very quickly</i>


00:39:07.380 --> 00:39:09.150
<i>because there was already sort of a tour</i>


00:39:09.283 --> 00:39:11.152
<i>that had to be done by the Yardbirds,</i>


00:39:11.285 --> 00:39:13.521
<i>which was a few dates in Scandinavia.</i>


00:39:13.654 --> 00:39:15.890
It was convenient that, lo and behold,


00:39:16.023 --> 00:39:17.692
there was this tour that we could do.


00:39:17.825 --> 00:39:19.827
<i>So we could go there
and have the benefit</i>


00:39:19.961 --> 00:39:21.394
<i>of playing in front of an audience</i>


00:39:21.529 --> 00:39:23.496
<i>away from London and all the rest of it.</i>


00:39:24.999 --> 00:39:26.801
<i>Off to Scandinavia we went</i>


00:39:26.934 --> 00:39:30.204
<i>for our first concert as the Yardbirds.</i>


00:39:30.338 --> 00:39:31.739
<i>And we went on an airplane,</i>


00:39:31.873 --> 00:39:33.406
<i>me and John sitting next to each other</i>


00:39:33.541 --> 00:39:34.842
<i>having a cigarette,</i>


00:39:34.976 --> 00:39:37.111
<i>seeing lots and lots of silverware,</i>


00:39:37.245 --> 00:39:39.280
<i>knives and forks in front of us.</i>


00:39:39.412 --> 00:39:41.215
Stuff that we would normally have stolen


00:39:41.349 --> 00:39:43.383
was there in profusion.


00:39:43.517 --> 00:39:45.553
Couldn't have even got
enough of it in our bags


00:39:45.686 --> 00:39:47.688
to get off the plane without
having somebody help us.


00:39:47.822 --> 00:39:50.091
It was great. Everything was there.


00:39:50.224 --> 00:39:53.393
Gin, tonic, people.


00:39:53.527 --> 00:39:56.163
People being kind and charming.


00:39:56.297 --> 00:39:58.900
Air conditioning. It was... Off we went.


00:39:59.033 --> 00:40:00.868
Because you couldn't...
What could you do?


00:40:02.402 --> 00:40:04.404
Couldn't go back to Mommy then.


00:40:09.710 --> 00:40:11.612
<i>When I told my colleagues</i>


00:40:11.746 --> 00:40:13.147
<i>and the people that employ me</i>


00:40:13.281 --> 00:40:15.116
<i>that I'm giving up session work,</i>


00:40:15.249 --> 00:40:17.385
<i>I'm gonna join a rock 'n' roll band,</i>


00:40:17.518 --> 00:40:20.221
everybody said, "You're mad.
You're completely crazy."


00:40:26.193 --> 00:40:28.596
On bass guitar, John Paul Jones.


00:40:28.729 --> 00:40:30.064
John Paul Jones.


00:40:34.235 --> 00:40:36.737
On drums, John Bonham.


00:40:41.474 --> 00:40:43.511
Lead guitar, Jimmy Page.


00:40:49.183 --> 00:40:51.052
And myself, Robert Plant.


00:40:55.089 --> 00:40:57.124
Yes! All right!


00:41:05.967 --> 00:41:06.968
Yes!


00:41:12.073 --> 00:41:13.808
Yeah!


00:43:40.888 --> 00:43:42.089
Yes!


00:45:43.410 --> 00:45:45.079
<i>It went so well, really,</i>


00:45:45.212 --> 00:45:47.281
<i>that, you know, the group
started to make the album</i>


00:45:47.414 --> 00:45:49.883
<i>straight after coming back
from Scandinavia. You know.</i>


00:45:50.017 --> 00:45:53.954
<i>How long had we been together
about then? What, a month?</i>


00:45:54.088 --> 00:45:56.658
<i>We went into Olympic Studios</i>


00:45:56.790 --> 00:45:59.460
<i>at night over a few weeks.</i>


00:45:59.594 --> 00:46:02.963
The very first time we go
in is the 25th of September


00:46:03.097 --> 00:46:04.365
in 1968.


00:46:04.498 --> 00:46:07.636
"Arrival time from 11:00 p.m. onwards."


00:46:09.403 --> 00:46:10.538
Let's take one.


00:46:10.672 --> 00:46:12.306
Right.


00:46:12.439 --> 00:46:14.743
There's Glyn Johns, dear Glyn,


00:46:14.875 --> 00:46:17.177
<i>at the controls of the Helios desk</i>


00:46:17.311 --> 00:46:19.748
<i>that was custom-built
for Olympic Studios,</i>


00:46:19.880 --> 00:46:21.616
<i>and he was doing the engineering on it.</i>


00:46:22.483 --> 00:46:24.519
Drumming set one.


00:46:24.653 --> 00:46:26.086
<i>John, Jimmy, and John Paul</i>


00:46:26.220 --> 00:46:29.923
<i>were much more connected in
these sessions than I was,</i>


00:46:30.057 --> 00:46:33.260
<i>because they were recording it live.</i>


00:46:33.394 --> 00:46:35.262
<i>They had to make it work on the floor.</i>


00:46:35.396 --> 00:46:36.598
<i>A take is a take.</i>


00:46:36.731 --> 00:46:39.333
<i>So it was an electric atmosphere.</i>


00:46:39.466 --> 00:46:41.935
<i>And I just tried to
land on top of it all.</i>


00:46:43.971 --> 00:46:46.641
<i>John Paul Jones brought the
most amazing bass playing,</i>


00:46:46.775 --> 00:46:50.044
and he was quite a force to
be reckoned with, musically.


00:46:55.249 --> 00:46:57.686
I was very much in love
with John's right foot.


00:46:59.019 --> 00:47:01.623
I had great respect for it.


00:47:01.756 --> 00:47:04.291
<i>And I used to try and highlight it</i>


00:47:04.425 --> 00:47:06.193
<i>and help it along</i>


00:47:06.327 --> 00:47:08.929
and give it space.


00:47:09.063 --> 00:47:10.431
Uh, take three.


00:47:10.565 --> 00:47:13.500
So I could be doing a riff
and just leave a note out


00:47:13.635 --> 00:47:15.670
and something of his would pop through


00:47:15.804 --> 00:47:18.205
<i>and the rhythm would
just suddenly come alive.</i>


00:50:06.608 --> 00:50:08.776
Well, I was gonna throw
everything I could at it.


00:50:08.910 --> 00:50:10.879
You know, I was gonna
play acoustic guitar,


00:50:11.011 --> 00:50:13.313
I played pedal steel guitar,


00:50:13.447 --> 00:50:16.183
I played slide guitar, electric guitar.


00:50:16.316 --> 00:50:18.786
<i>I wanted to have so
many different textures</i>


00:50:18.920 --> 00:50:21.523
<i>and moods on it that the whole album</i>


00:50:21.656 --> 00:50:24.859
<i>would just totally arrest the listeners</i>


00:50:24.993 --> 00:50:29.931
and take them to a realm that
they hadn't been in before.


00:50:30.063 --> 00:50:31.666
<i>The guitar's not on a regular tuning</i>


00:50:31.799 --> 00:50:33.066
<i>on Black Mountain Side.</i>


00:50:33.200 --> 00:50:35.003
<i>It is very much like a sitar tuning.</i>


00:50:40.775 --> 00:50:41.976
<i>Jimmy and John Paul</i>


00:50:42.109 --> 00:50:44.512
<i>were really at the top of their game.</i>


00:50:44.646 --> 00:50:48.315
<i>They'd played on so many
diverse pieces of music</i>


00:50:48.448 --> 00:50:50.652
that there was nowhere that
they couldn't find themselves


00:50:50.785 --> 00:50:52.152
comfortably going to.


00:50:52.286 --> 00:50:55.690
So that gave us a whole
magnificent bunch of colors


00:50:55.823 --> 00:50:56.758
<i>to play with.</i>


00:52:16.871 --> 00:52:18.740
<i>Jimmy produced the album.</i>


00:52:18.873 --> 00:52:19.974
<i>Which was great, you know,</i>


00:52:20.108 --> 00:52:21.241
<i>because it's got a different sound,</i>


00:52:21.375 --> 00:52:22.744
<i>and I like that.</i>


00:52:22.877 --> 00:52:25.079
He had all these ideas
of microphone placement


00:52:25.212 --> 00:52:27.582
to get distance, so he
knew what he wanted to hear.


00:52:28.983 --> 00:52:30.852
<i>There were some really complex things</i>


00:52:30.985 --> 00:52:32.687
<i>that went on during the mixing,</i>


00:52:32.820 --> 00:52:36.057
<i>like the backwards echo
that comes in You Shook Me.</i>


00:52:36.189 --> 00:52:38.826
<i>When you turn the tape
round and apply the echo</i>


00:52:38.960 --> 00:52:40.394
<i>and then turn it back over</i>


00:52:40.528 --> 00:52:42.563
<i>and then you get the
echo coming in reverse.</i>


00:52:55.810 --> 00:52:58.378
<i>I was cross-fading from
one song into another</i>


00:52:58.513 --> 00:52:59.914
<i>and all these sort of tricks,</i>


00:53:00.048 --> 00:53:02.382
so that when people
listened to the whole album,


00:53:02.517 --> 00:53:05.485
as I say, it would just
absolutely demand attention.


00:53:05.620 --> 00:53:08.488
Well, I mean, it was amazing. Yeah.


00:53:08.623 --> 00:53:11.491
I had a pair of those big
quad electrostatic speakers


00:53:11.626 --> 00:53:13.193
in my front room.


00:53:13.326 --> 00:53:15.863
You could sort of sit almost in them.


00:53:15.997 --> 00:53:17.865
And I just used to play it loud.


00:53:35.650 --> 00:53:38.553
If you finance the album yourself,


00:53:38.686 --> 00:53:40.521
you're in a much better position


00:53:40.655 --> 00:53:43.457
than if you're going in
with, like, a demo tape.


00:53:43.591 --> 00:53:45.225
<i>So, if you knew what you were doing,</i>


00:53:45.358 --> 00:53:48.730
<i>it made sense to complete the album</i>


00:53:48.863 --> 00:53:51.331
<i>and then go round and do a deal.</i>


00:53:55.302 --> 00:53:57.071
The American producer Bert Berns


00:53:57.205 --> 00:54:01.008
had really done a great PR
job for me at Atlantic Records.


00:54:01.142 --> 00:54:03.945
<i>When he took me there in 1964,</i>


00:54:04.078 --> 00:54:06.379
<i>and I said we should aim at Atlantic...</i>


00:54:07.215 --> 00:54:08.482
So that's what we did.


00:54:17.191 --> 00:54:19.794
<i>Yeah. Yeah. We'd done our bit,</i>


00:54:19.927 --> 00:54:22.262
and Jimmy and Peter were
going to convince people


00:54:22.395 --> 00:54:24.364
that they really needed it.


00:54:24.498 --> 00:54:28.236
Jimmy had the kind of keys to
the beginnings of a kingdom.


00:54:28.368 --> 00:54:30.571
And he went off to New York with Peter,


00:54:30.705 --> 00:54:32.573
and through them, they
got to see Jerry Wexler.


00:55:01.434 --> 00:55:03.638
We put it on, and
that was a great moment


00:55:03.771 --> 00:55:06.306
to actually be able to play it,
regardless of what they thought,


00:55:06.439 --> 00:55:10.645
to actually be playing it
to, you know, Jerry Wexler.


00:55:39.006 --> 00:55:41.341
<i>Atlantic wasn't allowed
to remix anything.</i>


00:55:41.474 --> 00:55:42.910
It was perfect. It was done.


00:55:43.044 --> 00:55:45.546
The sequencing was perfect.
There was nothing...


00:55:45.680 --> 00:55:47.748
Certainly no singles are
gonna be taken off it.


00:55:50.483 --> 00:55:54.021
Having seen a situation whereby singles


00:55:54.155 --> 00:55:56.290
have broken the spirit of a band,


00:55:56.423 --> 00:55:59.026
I didn't want it to break
the spirit of this band.


00:55:59.160 --> 00:56:02.964
And I made a point of
saying, "We're an album band.


00:56:03.097 --> 00:56:05.633
"We're not doing singles."


00:56:05.766 --> 00:56:07.935
<i>We could call the shots
because we owned the album.</i>


00:56:13.574 --> 00:56:16.344
And here I am, signing a contract.


00:56:16.476 --> 00:56:19.247
<i>Then there's Peter, looking really cool.</i>


00:56:19.379 --> 00:56:21.716
Looking quite like a Mafia don.


00:56:41.569 --> 00:56:43.470
<i>Peter Grant and Jimmy had fixed it</i>


00:56:43.604 --> 00:56:49.076
so that Atlantic had no say
in what we did whatsoever.


00:56:49.210 --> 00:56:51.612
They went there saying,
"Look, you want this band,


00:56:51.746 --> 00:56:54.382
"and we're gonna give
you the music." But...


00:56:55.816 --> 00:56:58.352
Peter Grant wouldn't
let any record executives


00:56:58.485 --> 00:57:00.054
<i>anywhere near the studio.</i>


00:57:00.588 --> 00:57:01.923
<i>Or the band.</i>


00:57:02.056 --> 00:57:04.457
<i>And they were terrified of
him, which was brilliant.</i>


00:57:04.592 --> 00:57:06.861
To have that freedom...
I mean, it was unheard of.


00:57:11.799 --> 00:57:13.768
<i>Rather than keep the Yardbirds name,</i>


00:57:13.901 --> 00:57:16.137
<i>the group became strong
enough to start fresh,</i>


00:57:16.270 --> 00:57:18.139
<i>and it was a decision
that we'd change the name.</i>


00:57:18.272 --> 00:57:21.242
<i>Keith Moon had suggested
a name, Led Zeppelin.</i>


00:57:21.375 --> 00:57:23.778
I thought that'd be a great name to use.


00:57:23.911 --> 00:57:26.614
I thought, "Yeah.
That's a terrible name.


00:57:26.747 --> 00:57:29.817
"Nobody's gonna remember that."


00:57:29.951 --> 00:57:31.652
But I couldn't come
up with a better one,


00:57:31.786 --> 00:57:33.287
so Led Zeppelin we were.


00:57:36.123 --> 00:57:39.994
<i>So, suddenly I was a dad
and I was nearly married.</i>


00:57:40.127 --> 00:57:42.430
<i>And I was in this group
that was now gonna be called</i>


00:57:42.563 --> 00:57:44.131
<i>Led Zeppelin.</i>


00:57:44.265 --> 00:57:47.868
<i>So I went back to my
parents with an open heart.</i>


00:57:48.002 --> 00:57:50.204
I called up my mom and dad and said,


00:57:50.338 --> 00:57:53.473
"You might like to meet your possible,


00:57:53.607 --> 00:57:56.844
"most probable,
daughter-in-law-to-be."


00:57:56.978 --> 00:57:58.579
And so, of course,


00:58:00.214 --> 00:58:01.349
they opened the door.


00:58:04.785 --> 00:58:09.690
<i>November the 9th, 1968,
we played Middle Earth.</i>


00:58:09.824 --> 00:58:13.394
<i>And just to celebrate the
fact that we did that gig,</i>


00:58:13.527 --> 00:58:15.663
I got married on the same day.


00:58:15.796 --> 00:58:18.733
Trying to get as many
things in in a day as I can.


00:58:19.967 --> 00:58:21.736
The car broke down
on the way to the gig.


00:58:21.869 --> 00:58:23.371
All that stuff, you know.


00:58:23.504 --> 00:58:25.373
<i>We'd figured out what we were gonna play</i>


00:58:25.506 --> 00:58:26.907
<i>and how we were gonna play it.</i>


00:58:27.041 --> 00:58:29.477
<i>And the first three numbers
were just bam, bam, bam.</i>


00:58:29.610 --> 00:58:33.047
You could see people going,
"What on earth is going on?"


01:01:31.626 --> 01:01:33.060
<i>It's a funny thing, you know.</i>


01:01:33.194 --> 01:01:34.728
<i>You couldn't play big gigs in England.</i>


01:01:34.862 --> 01:01:36.197
<i>You know, you'd say,</i>


01:01:36.330 --> 01:01:37.666
<i>"Do you want to take some
dates on Led Zeppelin?"</i>


01:01:37.798 --> 01:01:39.066
<i>and all this sort of thing.</i>


01:01:39.200 --> 01:01:41.235
<i>People wouldn't even book the band.</i>


01:01:42.203 --> 01:01:44.305
<i>So Atlantic decided that</i>


01:01:44.438 --> 01:01:47.374
<i>the record is gonna come
out on the 12th of January,</i>


01:01:47.509 --> 01:01:49.210
<i>but only in America.</i>


01:01:49.343 --> 01:01:52.213
<i>So Peter gets a tour together for us.</i>


01:01:53.914 --> 01:01:56.083
It was Christmas Eve, I
think, we had to leave.


01:01:56.217 --> 01:01:58.385
<i>You suddenly find the album's coming out</i>


01:01:58.520 --> 01:02:01.922
<i>so we sort of had to
go to America to play.</i>


01:02:02.056 --> 01:02:04.825
<i>I was concerned I wouldn't
feel home there at all.</i>


01:02:06.060 --> 01:02:07.094
<i>Pat kept saying,</i>


01:02:07.228 --> 01:02:08.996
<i>"I told you not to work with him."</i>


01:02:09.129 --> 01:02:12.399
<i>But she and my wife Maureen
were really good friends,</i>


01:02:12.534 --> 01:02:16.370
so they had the mutual
overview of celebration


01:02:16.503 --> 01:02:19.674
for what John was doing
and what I was doing.


01:02:19.807 --> 01:02:21.208
<i>We just rolled with it.</i>


01:02:22.943 --> 01:02:26.447
<i>Going to America was my
dream, for every reason.</i>


01:02:35.990 --> 01:02:39.893
<i>We're supporting Vanilla
Fudge to begin with,</i>


01:02:40.027 --> 01:02:43.230
<i>and they were the only group
that took us under their wing.</i>


01:02:44.865 --> 01:02:46.735
<i>They're good friends,
because they were friends</i>


01:02:46.867 --> 01:02:48.202
<i>when we had nothing, you know?</i>


01:02:50.437 --> 01:02:52.072
<i>Carmine and John Bonham picked up</i>


01:02:52.206 --> 01:02:53.907
<i>such a great repartee.</i>


01:02:54.041 --> 01:02:55.175
<i>They used to watch each other</i>


01:02:55.309 --> 01:02:57.444
and mimic each other
and play little parts.


01:02:57.579 --> 01:03:00.147
It was a great exchange of
energies between everybody.


01:03:03.284 --> 01:03:05.419
<i>Denver was the first show.</i>


01:03:05.553 --> 01:03:07.321
<i>Like playing in an aircraft hangar.</i>


01:03:07.454 --> 01:03:09.156
<i>There wasn't that many
people there either.</i>


01:03:09.290 --> 01:03:10.791
<i>I thought, "Oh, boy."</i>


01:03:10.924 --> 01:03:14.128
But I said, "Look,
let's just group together


01:03:14.261 --> 01:03:16.397
"as though we're playing in a club,


01:03:16.531 --> 01:03:19.634
<i>"and just play to each other.</i>


01:03:19.768 --> 01:03:21.001
<i>"Don't worry about whether the place</i>


01:03:21.135 --> 01:03:22.771
"is half full or totally full.


01:03:22.903 --> 01:03:25.607
"Let's just play for
ourselves to get a feel."


01:03:38.653 --> 01:03:40.854
<i>From the handful of
dates with the Fudge,</i>


01:03:40.988 --> 01:03:44.858
<i>then we go to the Whisky a Go Go in LA</i>


01:03:44.992 --> 01:03:46.761
and then we're going to San Francisco.


01:04:09.584 --> 01:04:11.218
<i>The album is already being played</i>


01:04:11.352 --> 01:04:12.853
<i>on the underground radio</i>


01:04:12.986 --> 01:04:15.889
<i>and people are really curious</i>


01:04:16.023 --> 01:04:18.660
<i>to see this band that they're hearing.</i>


01:04:18.793 --> 01:04:22.697
So we were gonna go in
there, like, with a hatchet


01:04:22.831 --> 01:04:25.933
<i>and just play like we'd
never played before.</i>


01:04:26.066 --> 01:04:28.235
<i>Just throw everything
that we'd got into it.</i>


01:04:29.903 --> 01:04:31.606
<i>We're backstage at the Fillmore,</i>


01:04:31.740 --> 01:04:33.874
<i>and, um, Peter Grant says,</i>


01:04:34.676 --> 01:04:36.009
<i>"Well, whatever happens,</i>


01:04:36.143 --> 01:04:38.680
"if you don't crack it here, it's over."


01:07:15.035 --> 01:07:18.305
We knew after that one that
we were on the right track.


01:07:30.518 --> 01:07:33.053
<i>Everything is traveling like wildfire.</i>


01:07:33.186 --> 01:07:35.389
<i>The whole thing is just building.</i>


01:07:35.523 --> 01:07:37.357
<i>People were bashing
down the doors to get in</i>


01:07:37.491 --> 01:07:40.193
<i>by the time we played Boston Tea Party,</i>


01:07:40.327 --> 01:07:42.095
<i>'cause we were working across the States</i>


01:07:42.229 --> 01:07:45.733
from the West Coast to the East.


01:07:45.867 --> 01:07:47.902
<i>On FM radio they would play</i>


01:07:48.036 --> 01:07:51.204
one side of your record
and back announce that side,


01:07:51.338 --> 01:07:54.374
have one word from a sponsor,
and then play the other side.


01:07:54.509 --> 01:07:55.944
<i>People would just listen to it</i>


01:07:56.076 --> 01:07:58.580
<i>and they'd go, "And they're
playing in your town tonight."</i>


01:07:58.713 --> 01:08:01.616
And so they'd all come on down.


01:08:01.749 --> 01:08:03.417
Rock 'n' roll with Led Zeppelin.


01:08:03.551 --> 01:08:06.654
Robert Plant is here and I
know you feel real good now.


01:08:08.088 --> 01:08:10.157
I'm gonna put another
caller on the phone here.


01:08:10.290 --> 01:08:12.459
<i>- You're on the air, dear.
- Is this Robert Plant?</i>


01:08:12.594 --> 01:08:14.629
<i>- Occasionally, yeah.
- Oh, God.</i>


01:08:14.762 --> 01:08:16.898
<i>I want you to know that
I'm having heart failure.</i>


01:08:17.031 --> 01:08:18.098
Oh, good God.


01:08:18.231 --> 01:08:20.735
Don't tell me. You need a doctor.


01:08:20.868 --> 01:08:23.136
<i>I just want you to know
that you are so sexy.</i>


01:08:23.270 --> 01:08:24.572
<i>- It's unbelievable.
- Okay, dear.</i>


01:08:24.706 --> 01:08:25.974
- Thank you very much.
- Bless you.


01:08:26.106 --> 01:08:27.374
<i>- Next? Next?
- Hello?</i>


01:08:27.508 --> 01:08:29.476
<i>I was gonna go see you last night,</i>


01:08:29.611 --> 01:08:30.912
<i>but I couldn't get tickets.</i>


01:08:31.045 --> 01:08:32.747
<i>It was really good last night.</i>


01:08:32.880 --> 01:08:34.448
<i>What's your name? Oh, forget it.</i>


01:08:35.717 --> 01:08:36.618
<i>- Hello?
- Hi.</i>


01:08:36.751 --> 01:08:37.986
<i>Hi.</i>


01:08:38.118 --> 01:08:39.687
<i>Hi. Guess what. I think you're a fox.</i>


01:08:39.821 --> 01:08:41.154
<i>What's that mean?</i>


01:08:41.288 --> 01:08:42.790
<i>It means you're a
very good-looking man.</i>


01:08:42.924 --> 01:08:43.992
Far out.


01:08:44.124 --> 01:08:45.793
<i>I really dug the concert.</i>


01:08:45.927 --> 01:08:47.160
<i>I really liked it.</i>


01:08:47.294 --> 01:08:48.830
<i>I just really think you're a fox</i>


01:08:48.963 --> 01:08:50.531
<i>and keep on doing what you're doing.</i>


01:08:50.665 --> 01:08:52.667
<i>That's very nice of you. We will.</i>


01:09:25.600 --> 01:09:27.467
The anticipation was overwhelming.


01:09:27.602 --> 01:09:29.704
I mean, really overwhelming.


01:09:58.866 --> 01:10:02.036
To come back to England
with what we'd experienced,


01:10:02.170 --> 01:10:04.572
I just had to keep quiet.


01:10:04.706 --> 01:10:07.975
<i>I mean, over here, the
record wasn't out yet.</i>


01:10:08.109 --> 01:10:11.012
<i>So I just didn't have anything to say.</i>


01:10:12.613 --> 01:10:14.214
People would have laughed at me.


01:10:15.783 --> 01:10:18.186
So Bonzo and I used to
get together and go...


01:10:19.386 --> 01:10:22.389
"Well, what was that?"
"Well, I don't know."


01:10:22.523 --> 01:10:25.258
"What happened?" "I don't know."


01:10:25.392 --> 01:10:29.030
<i>Crikey. It was just
amazing. Just ridiculous.</i>


01:10:29.163 --> 01:10:30.765
<i>I mean, we came back from America</i>


01:10:30.898 --> 01:10:32.200
<i>and we'd gone through a big change.</i>


01:10:32.332 --> 01:10:34.001
<i>You know, we were feeling
really good, you know.</i>


01:10:34.135 --> 01:10:35.603
<i>But it does affect you, you know.</i>


01:10:35.737 --> 01:10:38.238
<i>I mean, think about
it. We're 20 years old,</i>


01:10:38.371 --> 01:10:41.142
<i>and suddenly there were drugs</i>


01:10:41.274 --> 01:10:42.810
<i>and there was a lot of girls,</i>


01:10:42.944 --> 01:10:45.813
<i>and so many characters that
suddenly arrive on the scene.</i>


01:10:45.947 --> 01:10:50.551
This huge sort of sub-subculture
that spins around fame.


01:10:50.685 --> 01:10:52.720
Especially fame in
its very early stages.


01:10:52.854 --> 01:10:55.422
'Cause that's the place
where you can smell


01:10:56.224 --> 01:10:57.792
something new coming.


01:10:57.925 --> 01:11:01.428
It felt good. As it might.


01:11:01.562 --> 01:11:03.664
Especially as we were
doing it without the press,


01:11:03.798 --> 01:11:04.999
who didn't seem to like us,


01:11:05.133 --> 01:11:07.300
the few reviews we did seem to get,


01:11:07.434 --> 01:11:09.771
<i>the obvious one being
the Rolling Stone review.</i>


01:11:11.506 --> 01:11:13.373
<i>I remember reading that review,</i>


01:11:13.508 --> 01:11:15.777
<i>thinking, "Who are they talking about?</i>


01:11:16.911 --> 01:11:19.213
<i>"Do they mean us?"</i>


01:11:19.346 --> 01:11:21.149
<i>A lot of those writers at that time,</i>


01:11:21.281 --> 01:11:23.117
<i>I don't know whether they
were studying the music</i>


01:11:23.251 --> 01:11:25.285
<i>to criticize it or whether
they were more concerned</i>


01:11:25.418 --> 01:11:28.756
<i>about the sort of social activities.</i>


01:11:28.890 --> 01:11:32.627
But do you really care about
other people's responses


01:11:32.760 --> 01:11:34.829
if you're playing like that?


01:11:34.962 --> 01:11:38.566
Well, the answer is,
unfortunately, yeah.


01:11:39.700 --> 01:11:42.804
That's where you can come unstuck.


01:11:42.937 --> 01:11:47.340
<i>Fortunately, as four guys,
we did not care that much.</i>


01:11:47.474 --> 01:11:50.778
<i>We were more caring about
how we turned ourselves on.</i>


01:11:50.912 --> 01:11:53.781
<i>The first album, it just
got ripped to pieces.</i>


01:11:53.915 --> 01:11:55.283
<i>Like, loads would say, "No.</i>


01:11:55.415 --> 01:11:57.018
<i>"I don't like Zeppelin's sort of music,"</i>


01:11:57.151 --> 01:11:58.553
<i>for some reason, you know?</i>


01:11:58.686 --> 01:12:00.487
<i>So we all thought, you
know, it was a bit of a joke.</i>


01:12:01.823 --> 01:12:04.559
We worked really, really hard,


01:12:04.692 --> 01:12:07.728
and people got it, and
you could see they got it.


01:12:07.862 --> 01:12:09.730
Then they told their
friends that they got it.


01:12:09.864 --> 01:12:11.331
"Get some too."


01:12:11.464 --> 01:12:14.836
The venues are jam-packed,
so what people are hearing,


01:12:14.969 --> 01:12:17.104
as opposed to what they're reading,


01:12:17.238 --> 01:12:19.507
is what really makes the band.


01:12:34.021 --> 01:12:36.290
<i>It was exciting to be
doing these concerts</i>


01:12:36.423 --> 01:12:38.559
and being able to have this communion


01:12:38.693 --> 01:12:39.827
with the four of us


01:12:39.961 --> 01:12:42.630
and be able to just change, you know,


01:12:42.763 --> 01:12:44.899
things with a sense of
humor or an intensity


01:12:45.032 --> 01:12:47.735
or a level of aggression or whatever.


01:12:47.869 --> 01:12:50.437
<i>And this is how connected we are,</i>


01:12:50.571 --> 01:12:52.773
<i>that we can be moving in any direction</i>


01:12:52.907 --> 01:12:54.175
and everyone's following.


01:12:54.308 --> 01:12:56.244
If the guitar's leading,
they're all following.


01:12:56.376 --> 01:12:58.346
I can just hold back and change this.


01:12:58.478 --> 01:13:02.783
<i>I mean, the improvisation is
just at an extraordinary level.</i>


01:13:02.917 --> 01:13:05.052
<i>With Dazed and Confused,</i>


01:13:05.186 --> 01:13:08.455
the intensity is building.
It's positively evil.


01:13:38.219 --> 01:13:39.220
Uh!


01:16:18.212 --> 01:16:20.147
Oh! Ah!


01:18:13.894 --> 01:18:14.862
Hey!


01:20:00.901 --> 01:20:02.403
<i>I knew what we had</i>


01:20:02.537 --> 01:20:05.439
<i>and I just really wanted to
knock everybody dead with it.</i>


01:20:05.573 --> 01:20:08.643
<i>I had a whole vision for the next album.</i>


01:20:08.777 --> 01:20:11.713
It was going to be very cinematic


01:20:11.845 --> 01:20:15.983
in its approach to how the
music is done in the first place,


01:20:16.116 --> 01:20:19.654
let alone what the overdubs
and the layers are gonna do.


01:20:19.788 --> 01:20:21.822
<i>So I called for a
rehearsal at the house,</i>


01:20:21.955 --> 01:20:24.626
<i>and we rehearsed two new songs,</i>


01:20:24.759 --> 01:20:26.060
Whole Lotta Love


01:20:26.193 --> 01:20:28.530
<i>and What Is and What Should Never Be.</i>


01:20:28.663 --> 01:20:31.198
Jimmy would sit down
with an acoustic guitar


01:20:31.332 --> 01:20:33.535
and we'd work the songs out.


01:20:33.668 --> 01:20:36.136
<i>He just comes in and
plays what he's written.</i>


01:20:36.270 --> 01:20:38.773
<i>And then we, all
together, suss it all out.</i>


01:20:38.906 --> 01:20:42.376
We went into Olympic Studios in April,


01:20:42.510 --> 01:20:46.180
<i>and we're just really excited
to be back in the studio again.</i>


01:20:46.313 --> 01:20:50.884
My songwriting capacity
is starting to kick in


01:20:51.018 --> 01:20:54.121
because of the color of the
playing that surrounded me.


01:20:54.254 --> 01:20:55.623
<i>So, with Whole Lotta Love,</i>


01:20:55.757 --> 01:20:57.826
<i>Jimmy had done some work on the chorus.</i>


01:20:57.958 --> 01:21:02.129
<i>He showed me his riff,
which was incredible, really.</i>


01:21:14.408 --> 01:21:15.510
<i>And for the verse,</i>


01:21:15.643 --> 01:21:17.411
<i>I just sang a Willie Dixon lyric.</i>


01:21:27.921 --> 01:21:30.457
<i>And, you know, it was like...</i>


01:21:30.592 --> 01:21:33.728
I was finding the best
bits of Black music


01:21:33.862 --> 01:21:35.764
and putting it through the wringer,


01:21:35.896 --> 01:21:38.198
one way or another.


01:21:38.332 --> 01:21:41.569
<i>I think there's no point
in having Whole Lotta Love</i>


01:21:41.703 --> 01:21:42.936
and then having another riff


01:21:43.070 --> 01:21:45.339
which was pretty much
the same sort of sound.


01:21:48.342 --> 01:21:51.912
<i>So, the thing with What
Is and What Should Never Be</i>


01:21:52.045 --> 01:21:55.249
is that it's got these
laid-back, gentler verses


01:21:55.382 --> 01:21:57.084
and then it comes in with...


01:21:57.217 --> 01:21:58.720
<i>I won't call them a power chorus,</i>


01:21:58.853 --> 01:22:01.188
<i>but it's more intensity to it,</i>


01:22:01.321 --> 01:22:03.223
<i>so that you could then illustrate</i>


01:22:03.357 --> 01:22:06.293
John Bonham's power and
the group's power as well,


01:22:06.427 --> 01:22:07.662
the whole thing.


01:22:26.113 --> 01:22:27.347
We had this tour coming up


01:22:27.481 --> 01:22:29.851
and I think it's gonna be really a hoot


01:22:29.983 --> 01:22:33.987
to be able to record in
various locations in America.


01:22:34.923 --> 01:22:38.058
<i>The idea was to be inspired by,</i>


01:22:38.192 --> 01:22:40.160
<i>you know, the love of the people</i>


01:22:40.294 --> 01:22:42.496
<i>and to get the reaction of the audience.</i>


01:23:21.435 --> 01:23:22.670
<i>It was just a joy</i>


01:23:22.804 --> 01:23:25.874
<i>to be able to go into
studios in the States.</i>


01:23:26.006 --> 01:23:28.408
<i>You've got the energy
of being on the road</i>


01:23:28.543 --> 01:23:30.410
and you're actually documenting it


01:23:30.545 --> 01:23:32.212
within whatever you're recording.


01:23:49.463 --> 01:23:51.799
John Bonham's kit was gonna be spread


01:23:51.933 --> 01:23:54.167
right across the stereo picture.


01:23:54.301 --> 01:23:56.069
So then you could have the placement


01:23:56.203 --> 01:23:58.907
of the instruments within
and the layering within.


01:24:05.547 --> 01:24:06.748
<i>He knew how to tune.</i>


01:24:06.881 --> 01:24:08.750
<i>He had the science of tuning the drum.</i>


01:24:08.883 --> 01:24:10.518
<i>So that when he hit it,</i>


01:24:10.652 --> 01:24:13.353
<i>it just resonated out of the top.</i>


01:24:13.487 --> 01:24:14.923
<i>It just projected.</i>


01:24:17.057 --> 01:24:18.960
And he played with his wrists.


01:24:19.092 --> 01:24:20.762
It's not all this forearm smashing.


01:24:20.895 --> 01:24:23.565
<i>But he could also do an
accent on the bass drum</i>


01:24:23.698 --> 01:24:26.099
<i>and you'd feel it, you know,
more or less in your gut.</i>


01:24:27.735 --> 01:24:29.971
<i>He was so inventive.</i>


01:24:30.103 --> 01:24:32.439
And inspiring to play with, you know.


01:24:32.574 --> 01:24:35.108
You'd hear the stuff and,
well, I don't wanna just go


01:24:35.242 --> 01:24:37.444
over that.


01:24:37.579 --> 01:24:40.147
<i>I want to do something that'll
fit in really well with it.</i>


01:24:40.280 --> 01:24:43.751
That's what I used to like
to do best, is to fit in.


01:24:43.885 --> 01:24:46.353
So everybody could be heard.


01:24:46.486 --> 01:24:48.121
That was the important thing for me,


01:24:48.255 --> 01:24:49.624
that everybody could be heard,


01:24:49.757 --> 01:24:52.359
what they were doing,
on a Zeppelin record.


01:25:47.280 --> 01:25:49.784
<i>We just recorded what we liked.</i>


01:25:49.917 --> 01:25:51.318
<i>I mean, Zeppelin always had</i>


01:25:51.451 --> 01:25:52.854
<i>completely different influences.</i>


01:25:52.987 --> 01:25:55.188
<i>We all listened to different things.</i>


01:25:55.322 --> 01:25:56.490
<i>And I always said</i>


01:25:56.624 --> 01:25:59.127
that Led Zeppelin was
the area in the middle.


01:25:59.259 --> 01:26:00.293
In between us all.


01:26:15.777 --> 01:26:18.980
<i>It was very encouraging to peel back</i>


01:26:19.113 --> 01:26:21.149
<i>the vulnerabilities</i>


01:26:21.281 --> 01:26:24.652
<i>of starting to create a
writing partnership with Jimmy,</i>


01:26:24.786 --> 01:26:27.187
<i>where we were breaking
down the sensitivities</i>


01:26:27.320 --> 01:26:29.023
<i>and the possible embarrassments.</i>


01:26:29.157 --> 01:26:30.925
Writing is a very intimate thing.


01:26:31.059 --> 01:26:33.027
You expose yourself.


01:26:33.161 --> 01:26:36.931
<i>I'd written some lyrics
on the first album,</i>


01:26:37.065 --> 01:26:38.533
<i>and also on the second album,</i>


01:26:38.666 --> 01:26:41.268
but I wasn't as
confident about my lyrics


01:26:41.401 --> 01:26:43.705
as I was about my guitar playing.


01:26:43.838 --> 01:26:45.973
<i>I had to start thinking about</i>


01:26:46.107 --> 01:26:48.308
<i>what do I do as a singer</i>


01:26:48.442 --> 01:26:51.045
<i>and as a guy who's gonna bring melody</i>


01:26:51.179 --> 01:26:53.181
or even a story to something?


01:26:53.313 --> 01:26:56.918
The idea was to inspire
him through the music


01:26:57.051 --> 01:26:58.452
to be inspired lyrically.


01:26:58.586 --> 01:27:01.488
<i>And then he came with
these lyrics for Ramble On.</i>


01:27:02.123 --> 01:27:03.825
Yeah, this is...


01:27:04.726 --> 01:27:07.962
<i>a rough shot of Ramble On.</i>


01:27:08.096 --> 01:27:11.199
"Leaves are falling all
around. Time I was on my way."


01:27:12.365 --> 01:27:15.870
That is the story of my life.


01:28:25.640 --> 01:28:29.510
I was really keen to have a
lot of texture and layering,


01:28:29.644 --> 01:28:33.147
<i>certainly on behalf of
the guitar, with Ramble On.</i>


01:28:33.281 --> 01:28:36.349
<i>It has these various
guitar parts that go in it.</i>


01:28:36.483 --> 01:28:39.554
<i>You know, there's, like, guitar rolling</i>


01:28:39.687 --> 01:28:42.023
<i>that is moving over some of the verses.</i>


01:28:42.156 --> 01:28:44.158
<i>Then there's the whole
texture of the solo.</i>


01:29:01.509 --> 01:29:05.112
It was just a joy to be
able to do these tracks


01:29:05.246 --> 01:29:08.316
and everyone do their
part and then come back


01:29:08.448 --> 01:29:12.019
and go, "Wow. What's he done with this?"


01:29:12.153 --> 01:29:14.121
<i>There were so many diverse places</i>


01:29:14.255 --> 01:29:15.723
<i>that it was coming from.</i>


01:29:15.857 --> 01:29:19.527
<i>The very fact that we were
able to be intimate in writing,</i>


01:29:19.660 --> 01:29:21.896
that's the big breakthrough,


01:29:22.029 --> 01:29:24.899
<i>because it's a total exposure.</i>


01:29:25.032 --> 01:29:28.936
As a kid, my parents took me
to every mystical mountaintop


01:29:29.070 --> 01:29:33.040
and every beautiful ruin and...


01:29:33.174 --> 01:29:36.911
<i>all that sort of great
resonance of another people.</i>


01:29:37.044 --> 01:29:38.713
<i>Well,</i>


01:29:38.846 --> 01:29:40.548
that's what that song's
all about, really.


01:29:40.681 --> 01:29:42.917
Me, you, and the other people.


01:30:23.324 --> 01:30:25.293
Gig after gig, night after night,


01:30:25.425 --> 01:30:28.296
maybe we had 20 songs to choose
from at that point in time.


01:30:28.461 --> 01:30:30.298
Soon we'd have 40.


01:30:30.430 --> 01:30:33.968
We'd opened a door to America
and there was a glint of light,


01:30:34.101 --> 01:30:37.271
and, obviously, you're gonna
sort of kick the door open


01:30:37.405 --> 01:30:40.308
and go in and really...


01:30:40.440 --> 01:30:42.310
It's a massive continent.


01:30:42.442 --> 01:30:44.345
<i>And we really wanted
to get the word across</i>


01:30:44.477 --> 01:30:45.947
<i>through live performances.</i>


01:30:49.650 --> 01:30:52.753
We toured horrendously at first.


01:30:52.887 --> 01:30:55.957
<i>We toured for months
on end, pretty much.</i>


01:30:56.090 --> 01:30:58.726
Three of these guys had families already


01:30:58.859 --> 01:31:00.493
at that point in time,


01:31:00.628 --> 01:31:04.131
<i>and this is our fifth
tour in seven months,</i>


01:31:04.265 --> 01:31:08.002
<i>so they were away from their
children for a long time.</i>


01:31:09.203 --> 01:31:11.572
It is difficult when
you've got a family.


01:31:11.706 --> 01:31:15.109
<i>I flew with my wife Mo
to all the good spots.</i>


01:31:15.242 --> 01:31:16.410
<i>She'd come to New York.</i>


01:31:16.544 --> 01:31:18.612
<i>She'd just take the kids shopping.</i>


01:31:18.746 --> 01:31:20.514
Go to shows. It was great.


01:31:20.648 --> 01:31:22.249
But then they'd go home and leave us


01:31:22.383 --> 01:31:24.752
to get on with touring
the rest of the country.


01:31:27.254 --> 01:31:30.691
<i>There weren't tour buses or
any niceties in those days.</i>


01:31:30.825 --> 01:31:33.194
<i>It was just like getting
whatever transport you can,</i>


01:31:33.327 --> 01:31:36.397
<i>get to the gig, play it,
then try and have Richard Cole</i>


01:31:36.530 --> 01:31:39.900
find the next venue, or
even the city sometimes.


01:31:40.034 --> 01:31:42.036
You could always tell,
when he was like...


01:31:42.169 --> 01:31:44.538
You'd get at a junction and
see him going like this...


01:31:44.672 --> 01:31:47.742
You'd go, "No. We're lost again."


01:31:47.875 --> 01:31:50.378
<i>It's so much easier
to tour around England.</i>


01:31:50.511 --> 01:31:53.247
<i>If you're in America, you're
all day in a hotel somewhere,</i>


01:31:53.381 --> 01:31:55.750
<i>having arguments with bloody, you know,</i>


01:31:55.883 --> 01:31:57.618
<i>turdheads and everything.</i>


01:31:57.752 --> 01:31:59.053
<i>You know, it does affect you,</i>


01:31:59.186 --> 01:32:01.689
<i>like, what's happened
previously in the day.</i>


01:32:01.822 --> 01:32:03.491
<i>It was all commercial airlines</i>


01:32:03.624 --> 01:32:05.393
<i>so you had to get up
early in the morning and go</i>


01:32:05.526 --> 01:32:09.964
<i>and sit in some airport waiting
for a plane to somewhere.</i>


01:32:10.097 --> 01:32:12.366
<i>But that's how everybody
got to hear us in America.</i>


01:32:41.629 --> 01:32:43.097
<i>We came back from America</i>


01:32:43.230 --> 01:32:44.632
<i>and everybody wanted to book the group.</i>


01:32:44.765 --> 01:32:46.133
<i>And it was like a
change of tune, you know.</i>


01:32:46.267 --> 01:32:47.568
<i>There was no publicity</i>


01:32:47.701 --> 01:32:49.937
<i>and, strangely, you'd
get to a British show...</i>


01:32:50.071 --> 01:32:52.239
<i>"How did these people
know we were coming?"</i>


01:32:52.373 --> 01:32:54.475
<i>We figured out it was
just word of mouth.</i>


01:34:00.808 --> 01:34:03.110
<i>I just liked the idea of the stimulus</i>


01:34:03.244 --> 01:34:05.112
<i>of travel and attention.</i>


01:34:05.246 --> 01:34:07.081
What do you know if you don't seek?


01:34:07.214 --> 01:34:08.749
You've got to go out
and have a look for it.


01:34:33.707 --> 01:34:35.577
<i>We were playing festivals in America</i>


01:34:35.709 --> 01:34:37.546
<i>two years after the Summer of Love.</i>


01:34:37.678 --> 01:34:40.014
<i>I know that really has
no real significance,</i>


01:34:40.147 --> 01:34:42.016
<i>but to me it had every significance,</i>


01:34:42.149 --> 01:34:45.819
<i>'cause I was in a place
that I really wanted to be,</i>


01:34:45.953 --> 01:34:47.021
<i>emotionally.</i>


01:35:22.723 --> 01:35:24.858
<i>I can remember John doing a sound check</i>


01:35:24.992 --> 01:35:26.661
<i>at Newport Jazz Festival</i>


01:35:26.794 --> 01:35:29.463
<i>and I could just see all
of James Brown's drummers</i>


01:35:29.598 --> 01:35:31.600
<i>coming out of different doors</i>


01:35:31.732 --> 01:35:36.237
<i>going, "What the...
What is going on here?"</i>


01:35:36.370 --> 01:35:38.772
And he's going...


01:35:38.906 --> 01:35:41.710
He's going, "Yeah. I know.
I know who's watching me."


01:35:41.842 --> 01:35:44.613
But it was great. You know.


01:35:44.745 --> 01:35:48.382
<i>It's what made Zeppelin
unique in that way.</i>


01:35:48.516 --> 01:35:50.384
<i>It's just the way he approached rhythm</i>


01:35:50.518 --> 01:35:51.620
<i>and what he did.</i>


01:36:51.412 --> 01:36:52.980
<i>We recorded all the songs</i>


01:36:53.113 --> 01:36:55.149
<i>for Led Zeppelin II on that tour.</i>


01:36:55.282 --> 01:36:57.619
<i>It was like a kind of hurricane.</i>


01:36:57.752 --> 01:37:01.021
<i>A whirlwind of energy and release.</i>


01:37:02.256 --> 01:37:04.693
We hadn't really had
a chance, obviously,


01:37:04.825 --> 01:37:07.394
with the amount of time that
we were spending in America,


01:37:07.529 --> 01:37:09.997
to concentrate that much at home.


01:37:10.130 --> 01:37:12.266
<i>So we came back and actually played</i>


01:37:12.399 --> 01:37:13.702
<i>our first British festival.</i>


01:37:13.834 --> 01:37:15.369
<i>Would you welcome, please, Led Zeppelin?</i>


01:37:15.502 --> 01:37:17.204
And, of course, you still wonder,


01:37:17.338 --> 01:37:20.040
is this gonna have any resonance?


01:37:20.174 --> 01:37:21.776
<i>Is this gonna bring it on home?</i>


01:37:35.422 --> 01:37:36.558
Wow.


01:38:07.020 --> 01:38:09.557
<i>I've seen a photograph
of the first Bath Festival</i>


01:38:09.691 --> 01:38:14.328
but to see moving images of it,
well, that's absolutely amazing.


01:38:14.461 --> 01:38:16.063
<i>That was probably the first festival</i>


01:38:16.196 --> 01:38:17.866
<i>that we'd done in Britain.</i>


01:38:17.998 --> 01:38:19.868
And it didn't rain,
so it was really good,


01:38:20.000 --> 01:38:21.034
and it was fun.


01:38:23.872 --> 01:38:25.205
<i>But how long do you want off</i>


01:38:25.339 --> 01:38:26.541
<i>in between a tour and a tour</i>


01:38:26.674 --> 01:38:27.908
<i>and a tour? Not long, really.</i>


01:38:28.041 --> 01:38:30.277
I mean, we had no idea of fatigue.


01:38:31.245 --> 01:38:33.447
<i>There's just energy and power.</i>


01:38:54.067 --> 01:38:56.003
<i>At 32 minutes past the hour,</i>


01:38:56.136 --> 01:38:57.806
<i>liftoff on Apollo 11.</i>


01:39:46.754 --> 01:39:49.022
<i>That's one small step for man,</i>


01:39:51.793 --> 01:39:54.495
<i>one giant leap for mankind.</i>


01:40:09.611 --> 01:40:11.144
I can just remember we were in a tent


01:40:11.278 --> 01:40:12.914
and somebody landed on the moon.


01:40:17.451 --> 01:40:21.021
<i>I mean, can you imagine
just being in America,</i>


01:40:21.154 --> 01:40:23.725
as a kid, more or less,
and just looking up...


01:40:23.858 --> 01:40:26.126
You got this thing
going on with the music


01:40:26.260 --> 01:40:27.896
<i>and you look up and
there's a man on the moon.</i>


01:41:09.537 --> 01:41:11.973
<i>You know, it's a big
old world out there,</i>


01:41:12.105 --> 01:41:15.643
<i>and I'm sure that 99% of
people are far more interested</i>


01:41:15.777 --> 01:41:20.347
<i>in the fact that these guys
got back safely from the moon.</i>


01:41:20.480 --> 01:41:24.217
<i>But the day they got back,
that was a big day for us too.</i>


01:41:32.994 --> 01:41:35.362
<i>That is the biggest deal</i>


01:41:35.495 --> 01:41:37.932
<i>if you're a kid and
you want to be a singer.</i>


01:41:38.066 --> 01:41:42.235
You get a gold record. I
mean, what happens next?


01:41:43.071 --> 01:41:44.471
Doesn't matter, does it?


01:41:49.544 --> 01:41:51.946
<i>I just really wanted to make sure that</i>


01:41:52.080 --> 01:41:55.315
<i>this album was just really
gonna get everybody excited.</i>


01:41:55.449 --> 01:41:59.087
<i>I wanted it to be something
that they hadn't heard before.</i>


01:41:59.219 --> 01:42:02.991
The acoustics and the
electrics and the more radical


01:42:03.123 --> 01:42:04.926
and even avant-garde.


01:42:05.059 --> 01:42:07.160
<i>So, when it came to doing the mix</i>


01:42:07.294 --> 01:42:08.963
<i>for Led Zeppelin II,</i>


01:42:09.097 --> 01:42:12.432
<i>I'd mixed it with Eddie Kramer
at A&R Studios in New York.</i>


01:42:13.467 --> 01:42:15.302
<i>I think he did an amazing job.</i>


01:42:15.435 --> 01:42:18.171
And, as was the case
with these mixing things,


01:42:18.305 --> 01:42:20.742
there was often more than
two hands on the desk.


01:42:23.911 --> 01:42:26.114
<i>I really wanted to do
something with Whole Lotta Love</i>


01:42:26.246 --> 01:42:28.315
to stop it being a single.


01:42:28.448 --> 01:42:33.186
So it was gonna have a
radical avant-garde section


01:42:33.320 --> 01:42:36.591
<i>in the middle that
began with a sonic wave.</i>


01:42:42.664 --> 01:42:44.532
<i>Then when I put the overdubs on it</i>


01:42:44.666 --> 01:42:46.333
for what was gonna go in the middle,


01:42:46.466 --> 01:42:49.169
I de-tuned the guitar all the way down


01:42:49.302 --> 01:42:53.373
and I was just pulling the
strings across and across.


01:43:01.849 --> 01:43:03.250
And then there was lots of panning.


01:43:03.383 --> 01:43:05.485
That means moving things
from left to right.


01:43:27.441 --> 01:43:29.342
<i>And so that was it.</i>


01:43:29.476 --> 01:43:31.813
<i>Led Zeppelin II was completed</i>


01:43:32.647 --> 01:43:34.347
and ready to be delivered.


01:49:09.884 --> 01:49:13.187
<i>January the 9th, in 1970,</i>


01:49:13.320 --> 01:49:16.257
<i>we played the Royal
Albert Hall in London,</i>


01:49:16.390 --> 01:49:19.427
<i>and if we're thinking back a year,</i>


01:49:19.560 --> 01:49:22.630
January the 9th, 1969,


01:49:22.763 --> 01:49:25.433
is when we were playing
in San Francisco.


01:49:25.566 --> 01:49:29.470
So this is a whole year's progress.


01:49:29.603 --> 01:49:31.038
<i>It was far out.</i>


01:49:31.172 --> 01:49:33.074
<i>I mean, I was just having a great time.</i>


01:49:34.543 --> 01:49:36.277
No chartered accountant here.


01:49:36.410 --> 01:49:38.879
This is like a London
concert, so, of course,


01:49:39.013 --> 01:49:41.182
this is where the families come.


01:49:41.315 --> 01:49:44.285
<i>Yeah, it's like a
gathering of the clans.</i>


01:49:44.418 --> 01:49:46.921
Just to go in there
and tread those boards


01:49:47.054 --> 01:49:50.458
was just something else,
you know? It was fabulous.


01:49:50.591 --> 01:49:51.725
And it was our own show,


01:49:51.859 --> 01:49:53.694
so we just went out there and did it.


01:49:57.264 --> 01:49:58.599
Led Zep!


01:49:59.366 --> 01:50:02.403
Led Zep! Led Zep!


01:50:03.538 --> 01:50:06.874
Led Zep! Led Zep!


01:50:07.608 --> 01:50:10.277
Led Zep! Led Zep!


01:50:11.612 --> 01:50:13.247
Led Zep!


01:54:53.661 --> 01:54:54.662
Thank you very much.


01:55:07.509 --> 01:55:10.911
Led Zep! Led Zep!


01:55:11.646 --> 01:55:14.883
Led Zep! Led Zep!


01:55:16.083 --> 01:55:19.086
Led Zep! Led Zep!


01:55:19.953 --> 01:55:21.155
Led Zep!


01:55:22.256 --> 01:55:24.057
My dad came to see that show.


01:55:24.191 --> 01:55:27.961
He was really proud, which was great.


01:55:28.095 --> 01:55:31.031
Said, "He's got great passion
and fire and all that."


01:55:31.165 --> 01:55:32.933
It was really nice.


01:55:34.201 --> 01:55:35.202
<i>John Paul and me,</i>


01:55:35.335 --> 01:55:36.838
<i>you know, we get on really good.</i>


01:55:36.970 --> 01:55:38.740
<i>I mean, we all do, you know.</i>


01:55:38.873 --> 01:55:40.608
<i>Each member of the group,
they're quite different,</i>


01:55:40.742 --> 01:55:43.243
<i>personality-wise, but
they're good lads, actually.</i>


01:55:43.377 --> 01:55:45.847
<i>They're, like, a category
on their own, really.</i>


01:55:45.979 --> 01:55:48.449
<i>Um, it was at least the middle of '69</i>


01:55:48.583 --> 01:55:51.084
<i>before I got to really
know each of them.</i>


01:55:51.218 --> 01:55:53.922
<i>It's a gradual thing, isn't it?</i>


01:55:54.054 --> 01:55:55.657
<i>I could talk about Robert forever</i>


01:55:55.790 --> 01:55:58.158
<i>because I know him so well,
really, that it's ridiculous.</i>


01:55:58.292 --> 01:56:01.663
<i>You know? And Jimmy's
pretty shy, actually.</i>


01:56:01.796 --> 01:56:05.032
<i>But knowing him like I have,
he's really great, you know.</i>


01:56:05.165 --> 01:56:07.501
<i>I like each of them
for what they're doing.</i>


01:56:07.635 --> 01:56:10.237
<i>And, you know, I always
love playing in the group,</i>


01:56:10.370 --> 01:56:11.972
<i>so I don't need to do anything else.</i>


01:56:14.609 --> 01:56:16.678
<i>We got there in the first place,</i>


01:56:16.811 --> 01:56:17.779
<i>on the first day,</i>


01:56:17.912 --> 01:56:19.814
<i>playing Train Kept A-Rollin',</i>


01:56:19.948 --> 01:56:21.850
<i>'cause we were drawn to that moment</i>


01:56:21.982 --> 01:56:23.383
and it worked.


01:56:23.518 --> 01:56:27.054
But all the preamble to
that was just out there,


01:56:27.187 --> 01:56:28.890
throwing it up in the air.


01:56:29.022 --> 01:56:31.826
So why should it ever stop?


01:56:31.960 --> 01:56:34.963
If you have something
that you know is different


01:56:35.095 --> 01:56:36.230
<i>in yourself,</i>


01:56:36.363 --> 01:56:37.832
<i>then you have to put work into it.</i>


01:56:37.966 --> 01:56:39.500
<i>You have to work and work and work.</i>


01:56:39.634 --> 01:56:41.168
<i>But you also have to believe in it.</i>


01:56:41.301 --> 01:56:43.638
<i>But as long as you can stay really true,</i>


01:56:43.771 --> 01:56:46.373
<i>your aim is true, you
can realize your dreams.</i>


01:56:46.508 --> 01:56:47.909
<i>I do believe that.</i>


01:56:48.041 --> 01:56:50.244
I believe that that can be done,
'cause this is what happens.


02:01:33.360 --> 02:01:34.461
<i>Thank you.</i>

